  var P3 = {
    // Discovery
    euclid: { cap: 'Alexandria, c. 300 BC', ll: [29.89, 31.2], tx: 'Scholar: you draw a card when he arrives.', fact: 'His Elements builds the whole of geometry, step by step, from a handful of plain starting points. It was copied, printed and taught from for more than two thousand years.', art: 'Euclid about 300 BC in a shaded colonnade at Alexandria, teaching the first proof of his Elements: a spare Greek man of about fifty with a short grey beard, in a plain wool cloak, seated on a stone bench with a papyrus roll held open between his two hands, reading the next step aloud and glancing over the top of the roll. On the paving at his feet a young pupil kneels, swinging a cord pegged at one end to draw the second of two crossing circles in chalk, the triangle between them already ruled. Two more pupils lean in. The bright harbour shows between the columns. Seen from the pupil\'s side, low, so that Euclid\'s face and the roll are right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. Nobody knows his face, so he is an ordinary Greek scholar of his day: no long white beard, no metal compasses with a hinge, no bound books. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only column and shadow there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    eratosthenes: { cap: 'Cyrene and Alexandria, c. 276 to c. 194 BC', ll: [29.905, 31.205], tx: 'Scholar: you draw a card when he arrives.', fact: 'He worked out the distance around the Earth from the length of a shadow in Alexandria at noon on midsummer\'s day, when the Sun stood straight overhead in a town far to the south. His answer was close to the true one.', art: 'Eratosthenes at noon on midsummer\'s day about 240 BC, measuring the Earth from a courtyard in Alexandria: a Greek scholar of about thirty-five, clean-shaven, in a light linen tunic with a cloak over one arm, down on one knee beside a bronze sundial bowl set on a low pillar, a hollow half-sphere with an upright pin at its centre. He is marking the tip of the pin\'s short shadow on the inside of the bowl with a reed pen, squinting against the glare, and his other hand shades his eyes. A boy holds an inkpot and a wax tablet. The sun is almost straight overhead, so every shadow in the white courtyard is small and sharp. Seen from the far side of the bowl, his face right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. Ptolemaic Alexandria: no telescope, no globe, no glass lenses. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only bare sunlit wall there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    hippocrates: { cap: 'Kos, c. 460 to c. 370 BC', ll: [27.29, 36.89], tx: 'Inspiring: your other Figures here get +1 Might.', fact: 'He and his school taught that illness has natural causes, and that a doctor should watch the patient closely and note what happens day by day. Many new doctors still take an oath that carries his name.', art: 'Hippocrates on the island of Kos about 400 BC, examining a patient in the open air: an old man with a bald crown and a short white beard, as the ancient busts named for him show, in a plain cloak that leaves one shoulder bare, bending with his ear laid flat against the bare back of a fisherman who sits on a stool, listening to the man\'s breathing with his eyes shut and one hand raised for quiet. Two students stand by, one with a wax tablet and stylus ready, the other holding a bowl and a sponge. They are in the dappled shade of a huge plane tree in a small harbour town. Seen from the side, from the waist up, the old doctor\'s head right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. Classical Greece: no serpent staff, no white coat, no surgery or blood. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only leaves and sky there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    zhangheng: { cap: 'Luoyang, 78 to 139', ll: [112.45, 34.62], tx: 'When you play a Relic, draw a card.', fact: 'In 132 he showed the Han court a bronze urn ringed with eight dragons, each holding a ball above an open-mouthed toad. When the ground shook far away one ball dropped, and it is said to have marked an earthquake days before riders brought the news.', art: 'Zhang Heng, court astronomer of the Han dynasty, about AD 138, at the moment his earthquake urn proves itself: a slender scholar of sixty with a thin grey beard, in a long dark robe with wide sleeves, a sash and the tall lacquered cap of a Han official, standing with one hand raised and his head turned sharply toward a great bronze vessel shaped like a wine jar, as tall as a man. Eight bronze dragon heads point down around its shoulder, each with a ball in its jaws, and eight bronze toads squat below with their mouths open. One ball is in mid-air, falling from a dragon into a toad. Two junior officials have dropped their bamboo slips and stare. A timber hall with red pillars. Seen from across the vessel, Zhang Heng\'s face right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. Eastern Han China: writing on bamboo slips and silk, no paper books, no chairs. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only dark roof beams there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    cailun: { cap: 'Luoyang, died 121', ll: [112.47, 34.66], tx: 'Scholar: you draw a card when he arrives.', fact: 'An official of the Han court, he reported to the emperor in 105 a way of making paper from tree bark, hemp, old rags and worn-out fishing nets. Paper of a rougher kind was older, but his was cheap and good enough to write on, and it spread across China.', art: 'Cai Lun, an official of the imperial workshops of Han China, in AD 105: a beardless man of about fifty, as a palace eunuch was, in a plain belted robe with the sleeves tied back, standing at a wooden vat of grey pulp and lifting out with both hands a square bamboo frame stretched with cloth, water streaming from it and a thin even sheet of wet paper lying on the cloth. He tilts it to the light to judge it. Behind him two workers, one old and one young, pound bark and rags in a stone mortar, and a single finished sheet hangs over a pole to dry. Seen from across the vat, from the waist up, his face right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture and the dripping frame at the centre. Eastern Han China: no printed pages, no bound books, no writing on the sheets. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only wall there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    brahmagupta: { cap: 'Bhillamala, 598 to c. 668', ll: [72.26, 25], tx: 'Scholar: you draw a card when he arrives.', fact: 'His book of 628 is the first we know that treats zero as a number like any other, with rules for adding, taking away and multiplying with it. It speaks of numbers below zero as debts, and numbers above it as fortunes.', art: 'Brahmagupta at thirty, in AD 628, an astronomer of western India, timing the rising of the Moon from a flat stone roof at dusk: a lean man with his hair tied in a knot on top of his head and a short beard, bare to the waist with a cotton shawl over one shoulder and a wrapped lower cloth. He sits cross-legged, sighting along a straight staff set upright on the parapet toward a huge moon just clearing the horizon, one hand lifted to give the signal. Beside him a pupil watches a water clock, a small copper bowl with a hole in its base slowly sinking in a larger basin, and keeps the count with pebbles. A bundle of palm-leaf pages tied between two boards lies by his knee. Seen from close by on the roof, his face right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. India of the seventh century: palm-leaf pages, no paper books, no telescope, no turban of later centuries. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only darkening sky there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    alkhwarizmi: { cap: 'Baghdad, c. 780 to c. 850', ll: [44.38, 33.34], tx: 'Legacy of Brahmagupta. Your Deeds cost 1 less.', fact: 'His book on solving equations, written in Baghdad about 820, gave algebra its name, from the Arabic word al-jabr in its title. The word algorithm comes from his own name.', art: 'Al-Khwarizmi about AD 820, on a canal bank outside the round city of Baghdad, putting his new method to work: a scholar of about forty with a full dark beard, in a long plain robe and a neatly wound turban, standing on the raised earth bank with a small wooden writing board held against his chest, a reed pen in his other hand, looking up from his reckoning to call a figure to two surveyors below who are stretching a knotted measuring rope across a field between stakes. A farmer and his brother wait at the field\'s edge for the answer that will divide their inheritance. Date palms, the canal, and far behind the wall of the city. Seen from the bank, from the waist up, his face right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. Abbasid Iraq: no chalkboard of modern symbols, no x and y, no Ottoman dress. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only pale sky there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    fibonacci: { cap: 'Pisa, c. 1170 to c. 1250', ll: [10.4, 43.72], tx: 'Legacy of Al-Khwarizmi. On arrival, gain 1 Spark.', fact: 'His book of 1202 showed the merchants of Europe how to reckon with the nine Indian figures and zero in place of Roman numerals. One of its puzzles, about a pair of breeding rabbits, gives the string of numbers that now bears his name.', art: 'Leonardo of Pisa, called Fibonacci, about 1202, in a merchant\'s counting house in Pisa: a man of about thirty with dark hair cut straight across the brow and no beard, in a long belted tunic and a soft cap, standing at a sloping desk and writing a column of Indian numerals with a quill, quick and sure, while he holds up his left hand to stop an older merchant beside him who is still pushing counters about on a lined counting board. The older man stares at the finished sum. Sacks, a pair of scales and bolts of cloth fill the room, and through the open arch behind stands the cathedral\'s new bell tower, only three storeys built and already leaning. Seen from across the desk, from the waist up, Fibonacci\'s face right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. Pisa about 1200: no printed books, no eyeglasses, the tower unfinished. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only stone wall there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    gutenberg: { cap: 'Mainz, c. 1400 to 1468', ll: [8.275, 49.998], tx: 'When you play a Relic, draw a card.', fact: 'A goldsmith by training, he worked out how to cast metal letters by the thousand, all alike, in a small mould held in the hand. About 180 copies of his Bible were printed, and nearly fifty of them still exist.', art: 'Johannes Gutenberg about 1450 in his workshop at Mainz, casting type: a man in his fifties in a plain dark gown with the sleeves rolled and a leather apron, a close cap on his head, standing by a small brick furnace. In his left hand he holds a little two-part mould of wood and iron that he has just snapped open, and a single bright new metal letter is dropping from it into his right palm; he peers at it closely. A ladle rests in the pot of molten metal, and on the bench are rows of finished letters, a file and a punch. Deep in the room behind, out of focus, stands the wooden press. Firelight on his face and hands. Seen from the bench, from the waist up, his face right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. No portrait of him from life exists, so the face is an ordinary craftsman\'s: no long forked beard, no fur hat. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only dark beams there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    amundsen: { cap: 'Norway, 1872 to 1928', ll: [10.75, 59.91], tx: 'Voyager. On arrival, gain 1 Spark.', fact: 'He was the first to sail the Northwest Passage, in a small fishing boat with six companions, and he spent two winters on the way learning from the Netsilik Inuit how to dress in furs and drive dogs. In 1911 he used what they taught him to lead the first party to reach the South Pole.', art: 'Roald Amundsen on 15 December 1911, aged thirty-nine, at the South Pole, fixing the spot with a sextant as his own account tells: a tall, lean Norwegian with a long weathered face, a large hooked nose, a heavy fair moustache white with frost and pale eyes narrowed against the glare, in a hooded anorak and trousers of reindeer fur made in the Inuit way, with fur mittens hanging from a cord around his neck. He kneels on one knee in the snow and looks through a brass sextant held in his bare hands, aimed down at a small flat tray of mercury set on a box, in which the low Sun is reflected. One companion, older and shorter, in the same kind of furs with his hood down and a full dark beard, crouches beside him with an open pocket watch and a notebook. Behind them stand one loaded sledge, a pair of skis planted upright and three sledge dogs, each different, one asleep, one sitting, one watching the men; each dog is about knee high to a man. The snow plain is flat and empty to the horizon. Seen from the snow in front of him, from the knees up, his head right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. Historically accurate for 1911: no ice axes or ropes, no motor sledge, no ponies, no flag in this picture, no row of men. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only pale polar sky there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    kepler: { cap: 'Prague, 1571 to 1630', ll: [14.42, 50.09], tx: 'Legacy of Copernicus. Scholar.', fact: 'Working for years through another astronomer\'s sightings of Mars, he found that the planets move in ovals, not circles, and travel faster when they are nearer the Sun. He also defended his own mother in court when she was accused of witchcraft, and she went free.', art: 'Johannes Kepler in Prague about 1605, at the end of his long war with the planet Mars: a thin, slight man of thirty-three with a pointed dark beard, in a black doublet with a small white collar, bent very close over a huge sheet of paper, for his eyes were weak, drawing with a pen the long oval path of a planet with the Sun set to one side of its centre. His free hand presses flat a stack of pages filled edge to edge with columns of figures, and more discarded pages cover the floor. The candles have burned down and grey dawn shows at a small leaded window; a brass quadrant hangs on the wall. Seen from across the table, low, his face right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. Prague in 1605: he has no telescope yet, no globe of the planets, no wig. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only wall there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    montagu: { cap: 'London, 1689 to 1762', ll: [-0.13, 51.51], tx: 'Voyager: she can walk to any Place.', fact: 'Living in Constantinople as the British ambassador\'s wife, she saw old women guard children against smallpox by scratching a little matter from a mild case into the skin. She had her own son treated in 1718, and back in London she pressed doubting doctors to try it.', art: 'Lady Mary Wortley Montagu in Constantinople in March 1718, having her son protected against smallpox: an Englishwoman of twenty-eight with dark hair, her face lightly pitted by the smallpox she survived and her eyelashes gone, wearing the Turkish dress she adopted and was painted in, a long fitted coat of patterned silk over a fine shift and loose trousers, a small jewelled cap. She kneels on a carpet holding her five-year-old son firmly on her lap, his sleeve rolled to the shoulder, her eyes fixed on the work. An old Greek woman in a dark headscarf bends over the boy\'s arm with a needle in one hand and half a walnut shell in the other. A window shows domes and cypress trees. Seen from close, Lady Mary\'s face right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. Ottoman Constantinople in 1718: no harem fantasy, no veils over faces, no doctor\'s instruments of a later age. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only wall there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    duchatelet: { cap: 'Cirey, France, 1706 to 1749', ll: [4.95, 48.33], tx: 'Legacy of Isaac Newton. Scholar.', fact: 'She translated Newton\'s great book from Latin into French and added pages of her own to explain its mathematics. She finished the work in 1749, only days before she died.', art: 'Émilie du Châtelet in the summer of 1749, working through the night to finish her translation of Newton: a tall woman of forty-two with a long, lively face and dark hair loosely dressed and unpowdered, in a loose silk morning gown with the sleeves pushed back, seated at a table crowded with candles. A great Latin folio stands open on a reading stand before her; she writes fast on a sheet with her right hand while her left holds a pair of brass dividers stepped across a diagram of an orbit. Finished pages lie in a deep pile at her elbow, tied in bundles with ribbon, and a cold cup of coffee stands forgotten. A tall window is black with night. Seen from the side, so that she is in profile from the waist up against the black window, her head right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. France in the 1740s: no towering wig, no court hoops, no chalkboard. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only dark panelled wall there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    jenner: { cap: 'Berkeley, England, 1749 to 1823', ll: [-2.46, 51.69], tx: 'Legacy of Lady Mary Wortley Montagu. Steadfast: he has +2 Might when he defends.', fact: 'In 1796 he scratched matter from a milkmaid\'s cowpox sore into a boy\'s arm and showed that the boy could no longer catch smallpox. The word vaccine comes from vacca, the Latin for cow, and in 1980 smallpox became the first human disease to be wiped out everywhere.', art: 'Edward Jenner in May 1796 in a Gloucestershire dairy, studying the hand of the milkmaid Sarah Nelmes: a country doctor about to turn forty-seven with a round, kindly face and his own brown hair tied back, in a blue coat, yellow waistcoat and riding boots, holding the young woman\'s right hand in both of his and turning it to the light from the door to look at the blisters of cowpox on her fingers. She stands in a cap and apron, patient and a little amused. Behind them a brown and white cow turns her head in the stall, and a milking stool and wooden pail stand in the straw. A leather pocket case lies open on a barrel. Seen from inside the shed, from the waist up, Jenner\'s face right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. England in the 1790s: no syringe, no white coat, no powdered court wig. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only dark timber there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    humboldt: { cap: 'Berlin, 1769 to 1859', ll: [13.4, 52.52], tx: 'Voyager. Inspiring: your other Figures here get +1 Might.', fact: 'For five years he explored South and Central America, measuring everything from the heat of the sea to the blue of the sky. In 1802 he climbed higher on the volcano Chimborazo than anyone on record had ever climbed.', art: 'Alexander von Humboldt on 23 June 1802, high on the volcano Chimborazo in the Andes, at the crack in the ridge that stopped him: a slim man of thirty-two with windblown light brown hair, in an ordinary town coat, waistcoat and thin boots cut to pieces by the rock, his lips and gums bleeding from the thin air. He kneels on a knife-edge of black rock and snow, holding a long glass barometer upright in its case and reading it with fierce attention, while a companion steadies the foot of the instrument and a third man writes the figure with numb fingers. Cloud boils below them on both sides and the white dome of the summit hangs close above. Seen from just along the ridge, from the knees up, his face right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. No ropes, ice axes or climbing clothes of later times, no fur. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only cloud there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    anning: { cap: 'Lyme Regis, 1799 to 1847', ll: [-2.93, 50.73], tx: 'When you play a Relic, draw a card.', fact: 'She was twelve when she and her brother dug an ichthyosaur out of the sea cliffs near their home, and she went on to find the first complete plesiosaur. She sold fossils for a living, and the learned men who bought them seldom gave her the credit.', art: 'Mary Anning in December 1823 on the shore below the dark cliffs of Lyme Regis, uncovering the first complete plesiosaur: a sturdy young woman of twenty-four with a weathered face, in a heavy wool cloak, a long plain dress with mud to the knees and a bonnet tied down with a scarf, kneeling on a ledge of grey shale just washed bare by a winter storm. With a small hammer and chisel she is freeing the long line of neck bones that runs across the ledge to a tiny skull, and she has stopped, hammer lifted, to look at how long the neck is. A basket of tools stands beside her and her small black and white dog sits watching. Grey sea, wet rock, a landslip of clay behind. Seen from the seaward side, her face right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture, the fossil running down to the lower left. England in the 1820s: no dinosaur shown alive, no magnifying lens, no trousers. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only cliff and sky there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    champollion: { cap: 'Paris, 1790 to 1832', ll: [2.337, 48.857], tx: 'Scholar: you draw a card when he arrives.', fact: 'As a schoolboy he taught himself one ancient language after another, among them Coptic, the last form of ancient Egyptian. In 1822 he worked out how to read hieroglyphs, which nobody had been able to read for some fourteen centuries.', art: 'Jean-François Champollion at noon on 14 September 1822, in his attic study in Paris, at the instant he understands the hieroglyphs: a dark, curly-haired man of thirty-one with a full face, in shirtsleeves and an open waistcoat, half risen from his chair so suddenly that it is tipping over behind him. In one hand he grips a sheet of paper copied from a temple wall, on which a ring of picture signs is drawn in ink, a sun disc first among them; his other hand is flat on the table among dictionaries and sheets of the same signs set out in rows. His mouth is open in a shout. Sunlight from a small window falls across the papers. Seen from across the table, from the waist up, his face right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. Paris in 1822: no pith helmet, no pyramids through the window, no mummy. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only sloping attic ceiling there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    nightingale: { cap: 'London, 1820 to 1910', ll: [-0.12, 51.5], tx: 'Inspiring: your other Figures here get +1 Might.', fact: 'In a British army hospital in the Crimean War she fought for clean wards, fresh air and decent food, and far fewer soldiers died. She was also a gifted statistician, and drew a new kind of chart to show that dirt and disease were killing more men than battle.', art: 'Florence Nightingale in the winter of 1854 on her night round of the army hospital at Scutari: a slender woman of thirty-four with a calm, firm face and brown hair parted in the middle under a small white cap, in a plain black wool dress with a white collar and a short dark cape. She carries the lamp she really used, a Turkish lantern of folded waxed cloth like a small concertina with a metal top and base and a candle inside, held low at her side so that its glow lights her face from below. She has stopped at a low bed in a long corridor and bends to lay two fingers on the wrist of a sleeping soldier. Two more beds can be made out beyond his, each with a different man in it, one of them awake and watching her, and the rest of the corridor is lost in the dark: no rows of beds or of repeated figures. Seen from the foot of the bed, from the waist up, her face right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. No brass oil lamp with a spout, no nurse\'s apron with a red cross, no starched veil. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only darkness there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    pasteur: { cap: 'Paris, 1822 to 1895', ll: [2.34, 48.84], tx: 'Legacy of Edward Jenner. Steadfast: he has +2 Might when he defends.', fact: 'He showed that germs spoil food and cause disease, and that gentle heat kills them, which is why milk is pasteurised. In 1885 his new vaccine saved a nine-year-old boy who had been badly bitten by a rabid dog.', art: 'Louis Pasteur in July 1885 in his laboratory in Paris, on the day the boy Joseph Meister is first treated: a grey-bearded man of sixty-two with a grave, anxious face, in a dark coat and a small black skullcap, his left arm held a little stiffly at his side. He stands with his right hand resting on the shoulder of a nine-year-old boy in a short jacket, whose bandaged hands and leg show the dog bites, and he watches without blinking as a doctor in the background draws liquid into a small glass syringe. On the bench beside them stand glass flasks with long bent necks and a tall jar in which a strip of dried tissue hangs from a thread. Daylight from a high window. Seen from the bench, from the waist up, Pasteur\'s face right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture, the boy lower and to the left. France in the 1880s: no white coats, no microscope held to the eye, no electric light. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only wall there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    bly: { cap: 'New York, 1864 to 1922', ll: [-74, 40.71], tx: 'Voyager. Swift: she can walk on the turn she arrives.', fact: 'A newspaper reporter, she went around the world alone in 1889 and 1890 with one small bag, by steamer, train, rickshaw and donkey, in seventy-two days. Two years earlier she had pretended to be mad to get herself locked in an asylum for ten days, and wrote about the cruelty she found there.', art: 'Nellie Bly in January 1890 on the last leg of her race around the world, crossing America by special train: a small, quick young woman of twenty-five with a fringe of dark hair, in the one dress she took, plain dark blue wool, under a long checked travelling coat, a peaked cloth cap on her head. She stands on the open rear platform of a railway carriage with one hand gripping the iron rail and the other holding up a small leather handbag, the only luggage she carried, laughing into the wind as the track streams away behind. On a prairie station platform slipping past, four people wave hats and handkerchiefs, each different: a stationmaster, a farmer and his wife, and a boy running alongside. Steam and snow. Seen from the trackside, from the knees up, her face right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. America in 1890: no trunk, no parasol, no bustle, no hot-air balloon (there is none in the book or in her journey). Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only steam and sky there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    tesla: { cap: 'Smiljan and New York, 1856 to 1943', ll: [-74, 40.71], tx: 'On arrival, the strongest enemy Figure here misses its next turn.', fact: 'He designed the alternating-current motors and power system that still carry electricity to homes around the world. On the lecture stage he made glass lamps glow with no wire leading to them.', art: 'Nikola Tesla about 1892, lecturing in a darkened hall: a very tall, very thin man of thirty-six with black hair parted in the middle and a neat moustache, in a black tailcoat and white tie, standing alone on the stage with his arms lifted and spread. In each bare hand he holds a long glass tube with no wire leading to it, and both tubes are glowing with a cold white light that is the only light in the picture, throwing his face and shirt front into sharp relief. Behind him, half seen, stand two tall metal sheets on insulated posts and a table of coils. Three listeners in the front row are dark shapes at the bottom edge, each different. Seen from the stalls, from the knees up, his face right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. In his lectures of 1891 to 1893 he lit tubes both ways, held in his hands and hung between the sheets; this picture shows them in his hands. No lightning bolts leaping about the room, no tower, no goggles. Correct anatomy, two hands, each holding one tube. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only darkness there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    einstein: { cap: 'Bern, 1879 to 1955', ll: [7.45, 46.95], tx: 'Legacy of Isaac Newton or Michael Faraday. He has +1 Might for each Legacy line on your side, up to +2.', fact: 'In 1905, while he worked as a clerk in the Swiss patent office, he published four papers that changed physics, one of them on relativity. He was twenty-six.', art: 'Albert Einstein in 1905, aged twenty-six, at his desk in the patent office in Bern: a sturdy young man with thick dark curly hair, a dark moustache and large brown eyes, in a checked wool suit with a stiff collar and tie, standing at a high sloping clerk\'s desk on which a patent drawing of some electrical machine is pinned. He stands as the photograph taken of him at that desk shows him, one hand resting on the desk and a pen in the other, and he has looked up from the drawing, lost in a thought. A tall window behind him shows the roofs of the old town. Seen from inside the office, from the waist up, his face right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. He died within living memory, so this is a respectful, recognisable portrait taken from the photographs of him in 1905, at this documented place, with nothing invented. He is a young man: no white hair, no rumpled sweater, no blackboard, no formula written anywhere. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only wall there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    turing: { cap: 'London and Bletchley, 1912 to 1954', ll: [-0.742, 51.997], tx: 'Legacy of Ada Lovelace. On arrival, the strongest enemy Figure here misses its next turn.', fact: 'At twenty-four he described, on paper, a single machine that could carry out any calculation, the idea behind every computer since. In the Second World War he designed machines that broke Germany\'s Enigma code day after day.', art: 'Alan Turing in 1940, on a night shift in a wooden hut at Bletchley Park: a young man of twenty-eight with dark hair falling over his forehead and deep-set blue eyes, in a tweed jacket with a pullover and a carelessly knotted tie, sitting sideways on a hard chair at a trestle table. He holds a long sheet of paper punched with rows of holes up against a desk lamp and looks through it, lining it against a second sheet. On the table are stacks of messages in five-letter groups, pencils, and a cipher machine like a typewriter in an open wooden box, with three small wheels at the top. Behind him his tea mug is fastened to the radiator pipe by a small chain and padlock. Blackout curtains at the window. Seen from across the table, from the waist up, his face right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. He died within living memory, so this is a respectful, recognisable portrait taken from the photographs of him at this age, at his documented work, with nothing invented. Britain in 1940: no computer screens, no modern keyboards, no uniform. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only board wall there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    // Statecraft
    cyrus: { cap: 'Persia, ruled c. 559 to 530 BC', ll: [53.18, 30.2], tx: 'Inspiring: your other Figures here get +1 Might.', fact: 'He built the largest empire the world had yet seen, and let the peoples he conquered keep their own gods and customs. A clay cylinder found at Babylon records, in his name, that he sent exiled peoples home.', art: 'Cyrus, king of Persia, entering Babylon in October of 539 BC without a battle: a man of about sixty with a full, squared grey beard and long hair, in a long pleated Persian robe with wide sleeves, a simple band of gold around his brow, walking on foot and bare-headed under the towering blue-glazed Ishtar Gate with its rows of golden bulls and dragons. He holds his right hand open at shoulder height in greeting, and his sheathed short sword hangs untouched at his belt. Four people of the city, each different in age and dress, are laying green branches on the paving in front of his feet. Behind him a groom leads his horse and two spearmen follow in soft caps. Nobody else is shown: no crowd lining the way. Seen from the street in front of him, from the knees up, his face right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. The Persia of his own day: no tall later crown, no winged figure, no chariot. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only blue glazed brick there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    qinshihuang: { cap: 'Xianyang, 259 to 210 BC', ll: [108.71, 34.33], tx: 'Guard: enemies here must clash with him first.', fact: 'At thirty-eight he had conquered every rival kingdom and made himself the first emperor of a united China, with one script, one coinage and one set of weights and measures. He was buried near an army of some eight thousand life-size clay soldiers, made with different faces.', art: 'Qin Shi Huang, First Emperor of China, about 215 BC, inspecting the clay army being made for his tomb: a heavy-set man in his mid forties with a sharp nose, a thin dark beard and a hard, searching stare, in long black silk robes with wide sleeves and a tall black cap tied under the chin, a long bronze sword at his belt. He stands at the edge of a deep earth trench and looks down at five newly finished clay soldiers that stand below him, life-size, their armour painted in bright red, green and purple and their faces in flesh colour, each one different in face, hair, build and stance: a kneeling archer, an officer and three foot soldiers. The rest of the pit is still bare earth. A craftsman kneeling beside the nearest figure has frozen with his paintbrush in the air. Timber roof beams are being laid over the far end of the pit. Seen from in the trench looking up, the emperor from the knees up, his face right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. Qin China: no dragon robe of yellow, no paper, no stirrups. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only raw earth wall there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    augustus: { cap: 'Rome, 63 BC to AD 14', ll: [12.486, 41.889], tx: 'Legacy of Julius Caesar. On arrival, enemy Figures here miss their next turn.', fact: 'Julius Caesar\'s adopted heir, he ended a century of civil wars and ruled Rome for more than forty years as its first emperor. The month of August is named after him.', art: 'Augustus in 29 BC, closing the doors of the temple of Janus in the Roman Forum to show that Rome is at peace: a slight, handsome man of thirty-three with a calm face and short hair that falls in small locks over his forehead, as his statues show him, in a white toga with a fold of it drawn up over the back of his head as a priest wears it. He stands on the temple step with both palms flat against one leaf of a pair of tall bronze doors and is pushing it shut; the other leaf is already closed. A few senators and a boy with an incense box watch from below, and behind are the columns and tiled roofs of the Forum. Seen from the foot of the steps, from the knees up, his face in profile right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. Rome at the end of the Republic: no laurel wreath, no armour, no purple robe, no marble colossus. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only bronze door and shadow there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    wuzetian: { cap: 'Luoyang, 624 to 705', ll: [112.44, 34.67], tx: 'On arrival, the strongest enemy Figure here misses its next turn.', fact: 'She rose from a junior place among the emperor\'s women to rule China, and in 690 took the throne in her own name. In all of China\'s history she is the only woman who reigned as emperor.', art: 'Wu Zetian in the year 690, aged sixty-six, on the day she takes the throne of China as emperor: a stately woman with a full, strong face and painted brows, her black hair piled high and set with gold phoenix pins, in layered court robes of deep yellow and red with very wide sleeves and a long trailing sash. She stands at the top of a broad flight of palace steps with her hands folded inside her sleeves, looking down without expression. Below her on the steps five officials in round caps and belted robes, each different in age and build, are pressing their foreheads to the ground, and one who has looked up too soon is caught in her gaze. The courtyard beyond them is empty paving: no rows of kneeling figures. Red pillars and a wide tiled roof rise behind her. Seen from the steps below, from the knees up, her face right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. Tang dynasty China: no Manchu headdress, no long nail guards, no dragon throne of later dynasties. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only roof eaves and sky there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    darius: { cap: 'Persia, ruled 522 to 486 BC', ll: [52.89, 29.93], tx: 'On arrival, gain 1 Spark.', fact: 'He ruled an empire that reached from Egypt to the Indus, and tied it together with a royal road about 1,700 miles long. The Greek historian Herodotus wrote that neither snow nor rain nor heat nor darkness kept its relays of mounted messengers from their rounds.', art: 'Darius, king of Persia, about 500 BC, on the half-built terrace of his new palace at Persepolis, receiving a royal messenger, drawn after the carved reliefs of his own palace: a tall man of about fifty with a long, squared beard curled in rows and hair bunched at the nape, in a long pleated robe with wide sleeves, a tall cylindrical crown with a notched rim, a long staff in his right hand and a lotus flower in his left. He stands at the head of a broad stone stair, looking down at a courier who has gone down on one knee below him, a young man grey with dust in a short belted tunic, trousers and a soft cap, holding up a small sealed clay tablet in both hands. Behind the courier a groom holds a sweating horse, which is larger than the men, in true proportion. Behind the king two stonemasons, each different, are at work on a half-carved column capital shaped like two kneeling bulls. Bare brown mountain behind, hard morning light. Seen from the stair below, the king from the knees up, his head right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. The Persia of his own day: no winged figure in the sky, no chariot, no Greek armour, no rows of guards. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only clear sky there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    eleanor: { cap: 'Poitiers, c. 1122 to 1204', ll: [0.34, 46.58], tx: 'Voyager: she can walk to any Place.', fact: 'She was queen of France, then queen of England, and ruled her own duchy of Aquitaine besides. In her late seventies she crossed the Pyrenees in winter to fetch a granddaughter who was to be the next queen of France.', art: 'Eleanor of Aquitaine in 1193, aged about seventy, gathering the ransom for her son King Richard: a tall, straight-backed old woman with a strong-boned face, her hair and throat covered by a white linen wimple under a narrow gold crown, in a long dark red gown with hanging sleeves and a fur-lined mantle. She stands at a table in a stone vault and sets her own seal ring into the soft wax on the lid of an iron-bound chest, pressing down hard, while clerks weigh silver cups, plate and coins on a great pair of scales and notch the count on wooden tally sticks. More sealed chests are stacked to the roof behind her. Candlelight. Seen from across the table, from the waist up, her face right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. England in the 1190s: no tall pointed hat with a veil, no ruff, no paper ledgers. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only dark vaulting there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    kublai: { cap: 'Khanbaliq, 1215 to 1294', ll: [116.39, 39.92], tx: 'On arrival, gain 1 Spark.', fact: 'A grandson of Genghis Khan, he became emperor of all China and built his capital where Beijing stands today. Marco Polo was amazed to find that people in his empire paid for goods with money made of paper.', art: 'Kublai Khan about 1275 in his palace at Khanbaliq, drawn after the portrait made by his own court painter: a big, heavy man of sixty with a broad round face, a thin moustache and a small pointed beard, his hair looped in braids behind each ear under a plain cap, in a wide white Mongol robe that crosses over the chest. He sits on a low broad couch spread with a tiger skin and holds up between two fingers a large oblong sheet of dark grey paper money stamped with red seals, weighing it against a string of bronze coins that hangs from his other hand. A Chinese official kneels with a lacquer tray of more notes, and a Persian minister and a Mongol guard stand behind. Red lacquered pillars and a felt hanging. Seen from in front, from the waist up, his face right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. Yuan dynasty China: no dragon robe of the Ming, no pigtail, no fur hat with horns. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only dark hangings there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    ibnkhaldun: { cap: 'Tunis, 1332 to 1406', ll: [10.17, 36.8], tx: 'On arrival, the strongest enemy Figure here misses its next turn.', fact: 'Shut away for four years in a castle in the Algerian hills, he wrote a book that asks why kingdoms rise, grow rich and fall, and looks for the answer in how people live and work together. In 1401 he was let down by ropes from the wall of besieged Damascus to bargain with the conqueror Timur.', art: 'Ibn Khaldun in January 1401, aged sixty-eight, being lowered by ropes down the outside of the city wall of Damascus to meet the conqueror Timur: an old North African scholar with a white beard and a calm, shrewd face, in the hooded cloak of the Maghreb over a long robe, a large turban on his head, seated in a sling of rope with both hands gripping the line above him and his slippered feet braced against the stone. He looks down past his shoulder. Two men lean out from the battlements above, paying out the ropes. Far below on the plain a few tents and tethered horses show in the first light, with the smoke of many more fires behind them, and three riders, each different, wait at the foot of the wall. Seen from level with him in mid-air, from the knees up, his face right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. Mamluk Syria: no cannon, no Ottoman dress, no scimitar in his hand. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only wall stone there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    nezahualcoyotl: { cap: 'Texcoco, 1402 to 1472', ll: [-98.88, 19.51], tx: 'At the end of a round, if you hold his Place, draw a card.', fact: 'As a boy he watched his father killed and lived for years on the run before he won back his city of Texcoco. As king he was a poet, a lawgiver and an engineer, and built a long dike that kept salt water out of the lake around the Aztec capital.', art: 'Nezahualcoyotl, king of Texcoco, about 1450, on the great dike he designed across the lake of Mexico: a lean man of forty-eight with a thoughtful face, his hair cut straight above the shoulders, wearing a long cotton cloak dyed turquoise blue and knotted at one shoulder over a loincloth with long embroidered ends, a pointed band of turquoise mosaic on his brow, gold ear ornaments and sandals. He stands on the half-built causeway, a double row of timber stakes packed with stone and earth, holding a knotted measuring cord, and looks along its line to where it runs out across the water. Canoes heaped with rock are poled in on one side; on the calm lake behind lie floating gardens and the white temples of a far island city. Volcanoes with snow on the skyline. Seen from on the dike, from the knees up, his face right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. Central Mexico before the Spanish: no feather war bonnet, no skulls, no horses or metal tools. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only sky there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    sejong: { cap: 'Hanseong, 1397 to 1450', ll: [126.98, 37.58], tx: 'Scholar. Inspiring: your other Figures here get +1 Might.', fact: 'King of Korea, he gave his people an alphabet of twenty-eight letters, so simple that a scholar of his court wrote that a clever person could learn it in a morning. Koreans write with it to this day, and keep a holiday in its honour.', art: 'Sejong, king of Korea, about 1443, working out his new alphabet at night with the scholars of his court: a broad, heavy man of forty-six with a full face and a short black beard, in the red royal robe with a round gold dragon on the chest and each shoulder, and a black cap with two small wings standing up at the back. He sits on a floor cushion at a low table and holds two fingers to his own throat while he sounds a syllable aloud, his eyes narrowed with poor sight, and with his other hand he points to a sheet on which a young scholar has brushed a few plain shapes: a circle, a short bar, an angle. Three more scholars in blue robes and tall black hats lean in, one mouthing the same sound. Paper lanterns and a folding screen painted with mountains. Seen from across the table, from the waist up, his face right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. Early Joseon Korea: no Chinese emperor\'s bead crown, no Japanese dress. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only dark screen there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    pachacuti: { cap: 'Cusco, ruled c. 1438 to 1471', ll: [-71.97, -13.52], tx: 'On arrival, look at the top three Domains of your stack. You may lay one of them at your Gate or your Outpost in place of the Domain that is there. Put the others under your stack.', fact: 'He turned the small kingdom of Cusco into the Inca Empire, and rebuilt its capital in stone cut so finely that a knife blade will not go between the blocks. His name means something like “he who remakes the world”.', art: 'Pachacuti, ruler of the Inca, about 1450, overseeing the rebuilding of Cusco in stone: a stern, broad-chested man of middle age wearing a knee-length tunic woven in a grid of small bright squares, a cloak knotted at the shoulder, a band wound around his head from which a red wool fringe hangs over his forehead, and large round gold discs set in his ear lobes. He stands with a hand on a huge many-sided block of grey stone that four men, each different in age and build, are easing into place with ropes, ramps and bronze bars, and he crouches a little to look along the joint where it meets its neighbours, so close that no light shows. A finished wall of the same fitted blocks runs away behind, with thatched roofs above it and steep green mountains beyond. Seen from the foot of the wall, from the knees up, his face right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. The Andes before the Spanish: no wheels, no iron tools, no feather headdress of the plains, no horses. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only mountainside there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    suleiman: { cap: 'Constantinople, 1494 to 1566', ll: [28.98, 41.01], tx: 'Law: enemy Figures here can\'t clash.', fact: 'He ruled the Ottoman Empire for forty-six years, when it reached from Hungary to the Persian Gulf. Europeans called him the Magnificent, but his own people called him the Lawgiver, for the order he brought to the empire\'s laws.', art: 'Suleiman, sultan of the Ottomans, about 1540, giving force to a new law: a tall, thin man in his mid forties with a long neck, a hooked nose and a short dark beard, as the envoys of Venice described him, wearing a very large rounded white turban and a long robe of patterned silk with sleeves that hang to the floor. He sits on a low cushioned seat in a tiled room of the palace and watches closely as a court scribe kneeling at a low desk draws, in gold and blue ink at the head of a long scroll, the sultan\'s great looping signature mark with its three tall strokes. A white-bearded judge stands by holding the rolled text of the law, and a casement shows cypress trees and the sea. Seen from beside the scribe, so that the sultan is seen from the waist up, his face right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. The Ottoman court of the sixteenth century: no fez, no curved sword in his hand, no harem scene. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only blue and white tiles there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    akbar: { cap: 'Fatehpur Sikri, 1542 to 1605', ll: [77.66, 27.09], tx: 'He has +2 Might while you have more Figures here than the other player.', fact: 'Emperor of most of India, he could not read, yet he kept a library of 24,000 books and had them read aloud to him every day. He called scholars of many faiths to his court to argue their beliefs in front of him.', art: 'Akbar, Mughal emperor of India, about 1580, listening to a night debate between the faiths in the hall he built for it at Fatehpur Sikri, drawn after the paintings of his own court: a sturdy man of thirty-eight with a moustache and no beard, a small flat turban and a plain white muslin coat tied at the side, seated on a low platform with one knee drawn up and his chin on his hand, listening hard. In a ring on the carpet below him sit the speakers, each in his own dress: a Muslim jurist with a book, a Hindu scholar with a mark on his brow, a Jain monk in white, a Zoroastrian priest, and two Jesuit fathers in black gowns, one of whom is on his knees making his point with both hands. Oil lamps in niches of red sandstone. Seen from the edge of the ring, the emperor from the waist up, his face right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. Sixteenth-century Mughal India: no Taj Mahal, no later jewelled turbans with plumes. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only carved red stone there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    voltaire: { cap: 'Paris and Ferney, 1694 to 1778', ll: [6.11, 46.26], tx: 'On arrival, gain 1 Spark, or 2 if the other player holds this Place.', fact: 'His plays, stories and thousands of letters mocked cruelty and unjust laws so sharply that he was twice locked in the Bastille. As a young man he made himself rich by spotting a flaw in a state lottery and buying up the tickets with a friend.', art: 'Voltaire about 1765, aged seventy, at work in his house at Ferney near Geneva, drawn after the sketches a friend made of him at home: a very thin old man with a long sharp nose, sunken cheeks, bright mocking eyes and a wide toothless grin, in a fur-trimmed dressing gown and a soft cap pulled over a short wig. He sits sideways at a small desk and writes fast with a quill, looking up with delight at something he has just put down; a secretary at his shoulder holds a stack of sealed letters ready for the post. Books lie open face down on every surface. The window behind shows a garden and, far off, snow mountains. Seen from the front of the desk, from the waist up, his face right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. France in the 1760s: no full court dress, no tall powdered wig, no guillotine or revolution. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only bookshelves in shadow there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    catherine: { cap: 'St Petersburg, 1729 to 1796', ll: [30.31, 59.94], tx: 'On arrival, push the weakest enemy Figure here one step toward its Gate.', fact: 'A German princess who came to Russia at fourteen, she learned its language, took the throne from her own husband in 1762 and ruled for thirty-four years. She wrote to the leading thinkers of France and bought their libraries.', art: 'Catherine at thirty-three, at about eight in the morning on 28 June 1762, arriving at the barracks of the Izmailovsky Guards on the day she seizes the throne of Russia: a woman of middle height with a high forehead, bright blue eyes and brown hair hastily pinned and unpowdered, in a plain black dress, for she was roused from bed with no time to dress. She has just stepped down from a hired carriage and stands very upright while five soldiers in green coats with red facings press around her, each different in age and build, two dropping to their knees to kiss her hand and the hem of her skirt, one throwing his hat in the air. A bearded priest pushes through with a cross held high. A drummer beats the alarm. Seen from among the soldiers, from the waist up, her face right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. Russia in 1762: no crown, no ermine, no guards uniform on her yet, no horse. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only barracks wall and sky there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    wollstonecraft: { cap: 'London, 1759 to 1797', ll: [-0.09, 51.55], tx: 'On arrival, the strongest enemy Figure here misses its next turn.', fact: 'Her book of 1792 argued that women seem less able than men only because they are given less schooling, and that girls and boys should be taught together. She wrote it in about six weeks.', art: 'Mary Wollstonecraft in 1791 at one of her publisher\'s dinners in London, in the middle of an argument: a woman of thirty-two with auburn hair worn loose and unpowdered, a direct gaze and a plain dark dress with a white muslin kerchief crossed at the neck. She has pushed back her chair and leans forward with one hand flat on the tablecloth and the other raised, a forefinger marking her point, speaking straight across the table to three gentlemen in dark coats, each different in age and build, one of whom has his mouth open to interrupt and another of whom has put down his knife to listen. A plain meal of boiled fish and vegetables, wine glasses, candles. A print hangs on the wall. Seen from the men\'s side of the table, so that she faces us from the waist up, her face right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. London in the 1790s: no powdered wig, no wide court skirts, no bonnet. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only wall there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    victoria: { cap: 'London, 1819 to 1901', ll: [-0.19, 51.505], tx: 'She can\'t be moved. She has +1 Might for each round she stays, up to +2.', fact: 'She became queen at eighteen and reigned for sixty-three years, while railways, telegraphs and steamships tied a quarter of the world\'s people into her empire. Her nine children married into so many royal families that she was called the grandmother of Europe.', art: 'Victoria at six in the morning on 20 June 1837, a month after her eighteenth birthday, learning that she is queen: a very small young woman with a round face, large blue eyes and light brown hair hanging loose down her back, in a white dressing gown with a shawl over it and slippers, standing just inside the door of a sitting room at Kensington Palace. Before her two men in black have gone down on one knee, an old archbishop and a court official, and the official is lifting his face to tell her the news. She holds out her hand for them to kiss, pale but composed. Early light comes through tall windows; a candle still burns on a table. Seen from behind the kneeling men, so that she faces us from the waist up, her face right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. England in 1837: no crown, no widow\'s cap, no black dress, no old queen. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only window curtain there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    stanton: { cap: 'Seneca Falls, 1815 to 1902', ll: [-76.79, 42.91], tx: 'Inspiring: your other Figures here get +1 Might.', fact: 'At a meeting she helped call in her own small town in 1848, she read out a declaration that all men and women are created equal, and asked for the vote. She worked for it for the rest of her life, and American women won it eighteen years after she died.', art: 'Elizabeth Cady Stanton at thirty-two, on 16 July 1848, drafting the declaration for the convention at a friend\'s parlour table: a short, lively woman with dark curls gathered at each side of her face, in a striped silk day dress with a lace collar, seated at a small round mahogany tea table. She writes with a steel pen, and with her left hand she holds flat a printed copy of the American Declaration of Independence, running her finger under a line as she changes its words. An older Quaker woman in a plain grey dress and white cap sits beside her, reading the last sheet, and a third leans over the back of the chair. An ink bottle, a tea tray pushed aside, summer light through muslin curtains. Seen from across the little table, from the waist up, her face right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. New York State in 1848: no sash, no placards, no white hair. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only wallpaper there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    thoreau: { cap: 'Concord, 1817 to 1862', ll: [-71.35, 42.46], tx: 'He has +2 Might while he is outnumbered here.', fact: 'He lived for two years in a cabin he built himself beside Walden Pond, to find out how little a person needs. He once spent a night in jail for refusing to pay a tax to a government that allowed slavery, and wrote an essay about it that was read around the world.', art: 'Henry David Thoreau at twenty-seven, in the spring of 1845, building his cabin in the woods by Walden Pond: a short, wiry man with a large nose, deep-set grey-blue eyes and rough brown hair, clean-shaven, in shirtsleeves, a waistcoat and patched trousers. He stands inside the bare frame of a tiny house, one room, and is fitting a rafter, holding it up with one arm and a borrowed axe in the other hand, his head tipped back to judge the line against the sky. Hewn pine timbers lie about, with a heap of second-hand boards and two old windows leaning against a tree. Through the pines the pond shines below. Seen from inside the frame, from the knees up, his face right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. New England in 1845: no beard yet, no log cabin of round logs (it was a framed and boarded house), no rifle. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only pine branches there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    anthony: { cap: 'Rochester, 1820 to 1906', ll: [-77.63, 43.15], tx: 'Legacy of Elizabeth Cady Stanton. Steadfast: she has +2 Might when she defends.', fact: 'In 1872 she walked into a polling place in Rochester and voted for president, which the law did not allow a woman to do. She was arrested, tried and fined a hundred dollars, and she never paid a cent of it.', art: 'Susan B. Anthony at fifty-two, on 18 November 1872, in the front parlour of her house in Rochester as a deputy marshal comes to arrest her for voting: a tall, spare woman with a firm mouth, her greying hair parted in the middle and drawn smoothly back into a knot, small wire spectacles, a black silk dress with a white lace collar and a red shawl over her shoulders. She stands facing him and holds out both wrists together, demanding to be handcuffed like any other prisoner, while the marshal, a young man in a tall hat and overcoat, shifts on the carpet, embarrassed, hat half raised. Her sister watches from the doorway. A horsehair sofa, a lamp, lace curtains. Seen from behind the marshal\'s shoulder, so that she faces us from the waist up, her face right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. America in 1872: no banner, no sash, no crowd. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only wallpaper and picture rail there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    king: { cap: 'Atlanta, 1929 to 1968', ll: [-84.37, 33.76], tx: 'Legacy of Henry David Thoreau. On arrival, enemy Figures here miss their next turn.', fact: 'A Baptist minister, he led marches, boycotts and sit-ins against laws that kept Black and white Americans apart, and was jailed for it about thirty times. In 1963 he told a quarter of a million people in Washington of his dream that children would be judged by their character, not their colour.', art: 'Martin Luther King Jr. at twenty-seven, on the night of 30 January 1956 in Montgomery, Alabama, on the porch of his own house an hour after it was bombed: a compact young Black man with a round, serious face and a thin moustache, in a dark overcoat over a suit and tie, hatless, standing at the top of the porch steps with one hand raised, palm out, speaking quietly. The front window behind him is blown in and the porch boards are splintered. Below him in the dark yard stand six of his neighbours, each a particular person of a different age, build and dress, angry, two of them holding a stick or a bottle, and as he speaks those two are lowering what they hold. The rest of the crowd is only shapes in the dark. Two white policemen stand uneasily at the edge of the lamplight. Seen from in the crowd, looking up, King from the knees up, his face right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. He died within living memory, so this is a respectful, recognisable portrait taken from the photographs of him at this age, at this documented moment, with nothing invented. Alabama in 1956: no podium, no microphones, no monument. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only night sky and porch roof there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    // Valor
    fuhao: { cap: 'Yin, died c. 1200 BC', ll: [114.31, 36.12], tx: 'She has +2 Might while you have more Figures here than the other player.', fact: 'A queen of China\'s Shang dynasty, she led armies of up to thirteen thousand soldiers, as questions scratched on oracle bones record. Her tomb was found untouched in 1976, with more than a hundred bronze weapons in it, among them four great battle-axes.', art: 'Fu Hao, queen and general of Shang dynasty China, about 1200 BC, before her army on the morning of a campaign: a strong woman in her thirties with a commanding face, her black hair coiled high and held with a row of long carved bone pins, in a robe of patterned silk with a wide sash and a collar of jade beads. She stands on a rammed-earth platform holding upright before her, in both hands, a huge bronze axe of command, its broad blade cast with two tigers facing a human head between them, green-gold in the early light. Below the platform stand four of her soldiers, each different in age and build, with bronze dagger-axes on long shafts and painted leather shields, and one two-horse chariot waits behind them. The rest of the army is only spear points above the morning mist. A bronze cauldron on three legs smokes beside her. Seen from the foot of the platform, from the knees up, her face right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. Bronze Age China: no iron, no crossbows, no riders on horseback, no later dragon robes. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only pale dawn sky there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    vercingetorix: { cap: 'Gaul, c. 82 to 46 BC', ll: [3.12, 45.71], tx: 'He has +1 Might for each of your other Figures here.', fact: 'At about thirty he joined the quarrelling tribes of Gaul into one army against Julius Caesar and beat him at Gergovia. Trapped at last in the hill town of Alesia, he rode out and gave himself up to spare his starving people.', art: 'Vercingetorix in the spring of 52 BC on the rampart of the hill fort of Gergovia, as the Roman attack on it fails: a tall young chieftain of about thirty with long fair hair swept back and a heavy moustache, no beard, in a shirt of iron mail over a checked wool tunic and trousers, a twisted gold ring around his neck and a cloak pinned at the shoulder. He stands on top of a wall of timber, stone and earth with a long iron sword pointing down the slope and his head thrown back, shouting to three chiefs of other tribes who stand on the rampart beside him, each different in age, build and dress, one of them under a boar standard, men who were enemies a month ago. Below, small and far down the steep hillside, four Roman soldiers are falling back in disorder. Seen from on the rampart, from the knees up, his face right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. Gaul in the first century BC: no winged or horned helmet, no bare-chested barbarian, no plaid kilt. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only sky and smoke there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    amanirenas: { cap: 'Meroë, ruled c. 40 to c. 10 BC', ll: [33.75, 16.94], tx: 'Raider: she has +2 Might when she attacks on the other player\'s side.', fact: 'Queen of Kush, on the Nile south of Egypt, she fought the Romans for about five years after they took Egypt. The peace she won fixed the border and freed her kingdom from the tax Rome had demanded.', art: 'Amanirenas, the Kandake of Kush, about 24 BC at her capital Meroë, drawn after the carved reliefs of the queens of Kush: a powerfully built woman of middle age, blind in one eye as a Greek writer of her time recorded, with the lid of that eye closed, in a long fringed robe with a tasselled sash over one shoulder, a close-fitting cap with a rearing cobra at the brow, heavy bead collars and wide gold bracelets, a broad-bladed spear in her left hand. She stands at the foot of a temple stair and points down with her right hand as two soldiers lower a bronze head, the head of a statue of a young Roman with inlaid staring eyes, into a pit dug under the lowest step, where every foot will tread on it. Small steep-sided pyramids stand on the ridge behind. Seen from across the pit, from the knees up, her face right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. Kush, not Egypt: no striped pharaoh\'s headcloth, no Cleopatra costume, no leopard-skin fantasy. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only temple wall there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    arminius: { cap: 'Germania, c. 18 BC to AD 21', ll: [8.13, 52.41], tx: 'On arrival, push the weakest enemy Figure here one step toward its Gate.', fact: 'A chief\'s son of the Cherusci, he had served as an officer with the Roman army and knew how it fought. In AD 9 he drew three legions into forest and bog in the rain and destroyed them, and Rome gave up its plan to rule the lands beyond the Rhine.', art: 'Arminius in September of AD 9, aged about twenty-six, at the edge of the forest on the morning he turns on Rome: a tall young German noble with reddish-fair hair combed to one side and tied in a knot above his temple, a short beard, and the mail shirt and long cavalry sword of a Roman officer of the allied horse, which he has been. He has reined in out of sight of the marching column, dismounted, and is pulling the red Roman officer\'s cloak from his shoulders with one hand to let it fall in the mud, while with the other he grips the forearm of an older chief in a wool cloak and trousers who has stepped from the trees. Behind the chief three warriors can be half seen among the trees, each different, with spears and long narrow shields. Rain, dripping oaks, wet ferns. Seen from between the trees, from the knees up, his face right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. No winged or horned helmets, no fur loincloths, no battle yet. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only dark leaves there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    trung: { cap: 'Mê Linh, died c. AD 43', ll: [105.73, 21.18], tx: 'Inspiring: your other Figures here get +1 Might.', fact: 'Trưng Trắc and her sister Trưng Nhị raised northern Vietnam against its Chinese rulers in AD 40 and took sixty-five towns. They ruled as queens for about three years, and Vietnam still honours them every year.', art: 'The two Trưng sisters, Trắc and Nhị, in the spring of AD 40 at the mouth of the Hát River in northern Vietnam, calling their people to rise: two young noblewomen of the Lạc people, the elder a little taller, with their hair wound up in cloth, in wrapped skirts and short jackets with broad woven belts, each with a short bronze sword at her side. They stand on a river bluff on either side of a great bronze drum that reaches to their waists, its flat top cast with a many-pointed star and rings of flying birds; the elder sister is bringing a wooden mallet down on the star with both hands, and the younger holds a spear upright and looks out over the water. Below them two long war canoes are pulling in to the bank, with five people in the nearer one, men and women, each different. Seen from just below the bluff, both sisters from the knees up, their faces right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. Vietnam two thousand years ago: no elephants in this picture, no silk court robes of later dynasties, no conical hats. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only river mist there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    zenobia: { cap: 'Palmyra, c. 240 to after 274', ll: [38.27, 34.55], tx: 'Raider: she has +2 Might when she attacks on the other player\'s side.', fact: 'Queen of the desert trading city of Palmyra, she took Egypt and much of the Roman East in two years and ruled it in her young son\'s name. Scholars were welcome at her court, and she was said to speak several languages.', art: 'Zenobia, queen of Palmyra, about AD 270, sending her army out to Egypt along the city\'s great colonnaded street: a dark-eyed woman of about thirty with a strong, intent face, dressed as the carved portraits of Palmyra\'s noblewomen show them, in a long embroidered tunic with a cloak pinned at the left shoulder by a large brooch, a jewelled band across her forehead under a wound headcloth, heavy earrings and many bracelets. She stands on the steps at the foot of a four-pillared monument with a soldier\'s helmet held in the crook of her arm and her other hand lifted in salute. Past her down the street, between tall honey-coloured columns, ride three of her heavy cavalry, each rider different in build and gear, men and horses alike covered in scale armour, with long lances; each horse is larger than its rider, in true proportion. No rows of repeated riders. Seen from the roadway, from the knees up, her face right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. Roman Syria: no chains, no Roman triumph, no belly-dancer costume, no veil across the face. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only column tops and sky there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    richard: { cap: 'Aquitaine, 1157 to 1199', ll: [0.16, 45.65], tx: 'Legacy of Eleanor of Aquitaine. Raider: he has +2 Might when he attacks on the other player\'s side.', fact: 'King of England for ten years, he spent barely six months of them there. He led the Third Crusade to within a day\'s march of Jerusalem and then turned back, judging that he could take the city but never hold it.', art: 'Richard, king of England, in June 1192 on a hilltop in Judea, within sight of Jerusalem and deciding to turn back: a very tall man of thirty-four with red-gold hair and a short beard, in a long shirt of mail with the mail hood pushed back on his shoulders, a plain linen surcoat over it and a sword belt, dusty and sunburned. He stands with his long kite-shaped shield lifted in his left hand so that its rim hides the far skyline from his own eyes, his head turned aside behind it, as an old story says he did, refusing to look at the city he could not take. Beyond the edge of the shield we can see what he will not: small pale walls and a dome on a distant ridge. Two of his knights wait behind with their horses, each different, silent; the horses are larger than the men, in true proportion. Seen from in front and a little below, from the knees up, his face right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. The twelfth century: no plate armour, no closed great helm, no three lions on the shield, no crown. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only hard blue sky there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    lapulapu: { cap: 'Mactan, Philippines, 16th century', ll: [123.97, 10.31], tx: 'At home he has +3 Might instead of +1.', fact: 'Chief of the small island of Mactan in the Philippines, he would not bow to the king of Spain. When Magellan waded ashore in 1521 to force him, Lapulapu\'s warriors met the armoured Spaniards in the shallows and Magellan was killed.', art: 'Lapulapu, chief of Mactan, at dawn on 27 April 1521, waiting on his own beach for the strangers to come ashore: a lean, hard man in the prime of life (his age is not recorded), his long hair tied back under a red cloth wound around his head, his chest, arms and legs covered in fine dark tattooed patterns, as the Spanish wrote of the islanders, with gold ornaments in his ears, a loincloth with a long embroidered end and a sash. He stands at the water\'s edge with a long narrow wooden shield on his left arm and a heavy single-edged sword that widens toward its tip held low in his right hand, perfectly still. Behind him stand four of his warriors, each different in age and build, with bamboo spears whose points are hardened in fire; the rest, drawn up in three bodies as the Spanish account says, are only small shapes far along the beach. Far out, beyond a reef that their boats cannot cross, a few men in steel helmets are climbing down into waist-deep water. Seen from the shallows, from the knees up, his face right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. The Visayas in 1521: no feather headdress, no carved masks, no grass skirt. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only dawn sky and palm fronds there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    musashi: { cap: 'Japan, c. 1584 to 1645', ll: [130.73, 32.8], tx: 'He has +2 Might when he attacks a stronger Figure.', fact: 'He fought his first duel at thirteen and, by his own count, won more than sixty without a defeat. Late in life he was also a painter, and in a cave he wrote down his way of the sword as The Book of Five Rings.', art: 'Miyamoto Musashi in April 1612, aged about twenty-eight, on his way to the most famous duel of his life, as the old accounts tell it: a big, rough-looking swordsman with unshaven cheeks and long uncombed hair tied back with a strip of cloth, in a plain dark kimono with the sleeves tied up by a cord across his back. He sits in the bow of a small fishing boat rowed by one old boatman, shaving the blade of a spare oar into a long wooden sword with his short sword, curls of wood falling into the water, his eyes not on his hands but on the shore ahead. On a small flat island across the strait a tiny figure waits on the beach with a very long sword, and banners flutter behind him. Low morning sun in the rower\'s eyes. Seen from the water in front of the boat, Musashi from the waist up, his face right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. Early Edo Japan: no armour, no ninja, no two swords drawn. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only glare and sky there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    zumbi: { cap: 'Palmares, Brazil, 1655 to 1695', ll: [-36.07, -9.17], tx: 'Guard: enemies here must clash with him first.', fact: 'He was the last leader of Palmares, a country of villages in the hills of Brazil built by people who had escaped slavery, which held out against Portuguese armies for most of a century. Brazil keeps the day of his death, 20 November, as Black Awareness Day.', art: 'Zumbi, leader of Palmares, about 1690, on the wooden wall of its hilltop capital in the forest of north-east Brazil: a strong Black man in his mid thirties with a short beard and a grave, alert face, in a loose cotton shirt, knee breeches and a cloth sash, a long iron-headed lance in his hand and a knife in his belt. He is on the fighting step behind a palisade of sharpened tree trunks, one hand shading his eyes, looking down the steep slope over the tops of palms to where a thread of smoke shows an army on the march. Along the wall three people, two men and a woman, each different, are passing up a bow, a basket of stones and a musket, and behind them lie thatched houses, a forge and fields of maize. Seen from on the wall, from the knees up, his face right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. No portrait of him exists: no chains, no loincloth costume, no crown. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only palm crowns and sky there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    nanny: { cap: 'Blue Mountains, Jamaica, c. 1686 to c. 1760', ll: [-76.52, 18.08], tx: 'Raider: she has +2 Might when she attacks on the other player\'s side.', fact: 'She led the Windward Maroons, people who had freed themselves from slavery and lived in Jamaica\'s Blue Mountains, where British soldiers hunted them for years and could not beat them. A grant of 1740 gave her and her people their own land, and her face is on Jamaica\'s five-hundred-dollar note.', art: 'Nanny, leader of the Windward Maroons, about 1733, on a ridge of the Blue Mountains of Jamaica above a column of British soldiers: a small, wiry Black woman of middle age with a fierce, still face, her head tied in a cloth, in a plain wrapped dress of coarse blue cotton with a knife at her belt and strings of beads at her neck. She crouches behind a rock at the lip of a gorge with a cow\'s horn raised to her lips, sounding the signal, her other hand held out flat behind her to hold back three fighters who lie hidden beside her, a man, a woman and a youth, so covered in tied-on leaves and branches that they look like bushes. Far below on a narrow path beside a river, five redcoats toil upward, tiny, looking the wrong way. Mist, tree ferns, wet green light. Seen from along the ridge, from the waist up, her face right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. Jamaica in the 1730s: no chains, no witch\'s props, no torches. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only mist and leaves there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    washington: { cap: 'Mount Vernon, 1732 to 1799', ll: [-77.09, 38.71], tx: 'Inspiring. Steadfast: he has +2 Might when he defends.', fact: 'He kept a ragged army together through eight years of war against Britain, then gave up his command and went home to his farm. After two terms as the first president he stepped down again, and set the custom that no president rules for life.', art: 'George Washington on 23 December 1783 at Annapolis, giving up command of the army now that the war is won: a very tall, big-boned man of fifty-one with a long, strong face and his own reddish-brown hair powdered and tied back, in the dark blue coat with buff facings and gold epaulettes of the Continental Army, buff breeches and black boots. He stands in the middle of a plain assembly room and holds out a folded paper, his commission, toward four seated congressmen, each different in age and build, who have kept their hats on; the paper trembles a little in his hand and his face is tight with feeling. Three people lean on the gallery rail above, each different, one of them in tears. Winter light from tall windows. Seen from beside the congressmen, so that he faces us from the knees up, his face right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. America in 1783: no white horse, no crossing of a river, no sword drawn, no white curled wig. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only gallery rail and wall there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    napoleon: { cap: 'Ajaccio and Paris, 1769 to 1821', ll: [2.31, 48.86], tx: 'Legacy of Hannibal or Julius Caesar. He has +2 Might while you have more Figures here than the other player.', fact: 'An artillery officer from the island of Corsica, he made himself emperor of the French at thirty-five and for ten years beat almost every army sent against him. The code of laws drawn up under him is still the base of the law in France and in many other countries.', art: 'Napoleon on the night of 1 December 1805, the eve of the battle of Austerlitz, walking through his army\'s camp: a man of thirty-six of ordinary height with a pale, full face and short dark hair, in a plain grey overcoat buttoned over a green uniform, a black two-cornered hat worn sideways across his head, his hands clasped behind his back. He walks along a frozen track between the bivouac fires, and as the soldiers know him they are leaping up, twisting the straw of their bedding into torches, lighting them at the fires and holding them high. Four soldiers are near enough to see, each different in age, build and dress: an old grenadier in a tall fur cap, a boy drummer and two infantrymen in greatcoats. Beyond them only points of torchlight run away into the dark on both sides of him, with no figures drawn. He looks at the men, half smiling. Long muskets, breath steaming. Seen from in front on the track, from the knees up, his face lit gold right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. France in 1805: no white horse, no hand tucked in his coat, no crown or robes. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only night sky there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    chiefjoseph: { cap: 'Wallowa Valley, 1840 to 1904', ll: [-117.2, 45.35], tx: 'Voyager. Steadfast: he has +2 Might when he defends.', fact: 'Ordered off their homeland in 1877, he and the Nez Perce travelled about 1,170 miles toward Canada with their families and herds, fighting off the United States Army again and again. They were stopped forty miles short of the border.', art: 'Chief Joseph of the Nez Perce in the late summer of 1877, leading the families of his people over a mountain pass on their long retreat: a tall, heavy-shouldered man of thirty-seven with a broad, grave face, his hair swept up in a high roll above the forehead with a long braid hanging in front of each shoulder, shell earrings, a striped trade blanket wrapped around him over a cloth shirt, leggings and beaded moccasins. He walks on foot at the head of a file, leading a spotted horse on which a small girl sits in front of an old woman, and he is looking back over his shoulder at those who follow, each different: a young mother with a baby on her back, an old man leading a pack horse that drags lodge poles, and a boy on foot. The rest of the line is hidden by fallen timber and sleet. The horses are larger than the people, in true proportion. Sleet is beginning; the peaks are white. Seen from the trail ahead, from the knees up, his face right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. The Plateau peoples in 1877: no feathered war bonnet, no war paint, no rifle raised. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only grey sky and pines there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    menelik: { cap: 'Ethiopia, 1844 to 1913', ll: [38.9, 14.17], tx: 'At home he has +3 Might instead of +1.', fact: 'Emperor of Ethiopia, he armed and united his country while European powers were dividing up the rest of Africa. At Adwa in 1896 his army of up to a hundred thousand beat an invading Italian one, and Ethiopia stayed free.', art: 'Menelik II, emperor of Ethiopia, at first light on 1 March 1896 above the valley of Adwa: a big, dark, bearded man of fifty-one with a broad pockmarked face, in the dress he wore on campaign, a black silk cape over white cotton robes and a wide-brimmed dark felt hat, standing on a rocky spur under a great red umbrella held by an attendant. He has a field glass lowered in one hand and with the other signals forward one small mountain gun that its crew of three is dragging into place. Beside him stand two of his generals, each different, in lion\'s-mane collars and embroidered capes with round hide shields, and one priest in white with a tall processional cross. Below, between strange steep towers of rock, there are only dust, mist and gun flashes in the valley, and a few tiny white-robed riflemen going down toward them. Seen from on the spur, from the knees up, his face right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. Ethiopia in 1896: no spears only, no bare feet and loincloths, no European crown. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only rock peak and sky there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    wallace: { cap: 'Scotland, c. 1270 to 1305', ll: [-3.94, 56.12], tx: 'He has +2 Might while he is outnumbered here.', fact: 'In 1297 he and Andrew Moray beat a larger English army at Stirling by waiting until part of it had crossed a narrow wooden bridge and then cutting that part off. A letter the two of them sent a month later, telling the merchants of Lübeck that Scotland was open for trade again, still exists.', art: 'William Wallace on the morning of 11 September 1297, on the wooded crag above Stirling, watching the English army begin to cross the bridge below: a big, rawboned Scot of about twenty-seven with a short brown beard and hair to the collar (nobody knows his face, so he is an ordinary man of his time and place), in a knee-length padded and quilted linen coat with a mail collar, a plain iron helmet with a brim hanging from his belt, wool hose and leather shoes, a long spear upright in his left hand and a small plain triangular shield slung on his back. He crouches at the edge of the trees with his right arm stretched back, palm down, holding his men where they are. Two spearmen wait behind him, each different, one grey-haired and one a boy. Far below, small, a narrow timber bridge crosses a looping river, and on it a thin file of riders, two abreast, is coming over toward the marshy meadow on this side; a castle stands on its rock in the distance. Seen from the slope in front of him, from the knees up, his head right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. Scotland in 1297: no kilt or tartan, no blue face paint, no two-handed sword on his back, no plate armour. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only leaves and pale sky there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    thorpe: { cap: 'Oklahoma, 1887 to 1953', ll: [-96.7, 35.5], tx: 'Swift: he can walk on the turn he arrives.', fact: 'A member of the Sac and Fox Nation, he won both the pentathlon and the decathlon at the 1912 Olympics. Before one event his shoes went missing, and he competed in two odd ones, one of them picked out of a rubbish bin.', art: 'Jim Thorpe in July 1912 at the Olympic Games in Stockholm, winning the last race of the decathlon: a powerfully built young man of twenty-five with thick black hair and a broad, determined face, in the white vest of the American team with a shield on the chest and white shorts, driving for the finish tape on a cinder track with his chest thrown forward. On his feet are two shoes that do not match, one larger than the other and worn over extra socks. Two other runners, each different, are well behind him. A brick stadium with a clock tower, its wooden stands only a soft blur of colour with no faces drawn, and two officials in straw hats beside the track. Seen from just past the finish line, low, from the knees up, his face right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. He died within living memory, so this is a respectful, recognisable portrait taken from the photographs of him at these Games, at this documented moment, with nothing invented. 1912: no starting blocks, no modern running shoes, no feathers or costume of any kind. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only stadium tower and sky there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    owens: { cap: 'Alabama and Ohio, 1913 to 1980', ll: [-83, 39.96], tx: 'Swift: he can walk on the turn he arrives.', fact: 'At the 1936 Olympics in Berlin, where Germany\'s rulers meant to show that their own race was the best, he won four gold medals. A German long jumper, Luz Long, made friends with him in front of the whole crowd.', art: 'Jesse Owens in August 1936 at the Olympic Games in Berlin, in the air above the long-jump pit: a slim, long-legged young Black man of twenty-two with a calm, open face, in the white vest of the American team with a red and blue stripe slanting across the chest and white shorts, caught at the top of his leap with both legs flung forward, his arms reaching past his knees and his eyes fixed on the sand far ahead. A measuring board runs along the pit, and an official in a white jacket crouches with a marker. On the grass beside the runway a tall fair-haired German jumper in a tracksuit is watching him. The huge stone stadium behind is only a soft band of colour, with no faces and no rows of heads drawn. Seen from beside the pit, low, his whole figure in the upper half of the picture, his head right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. He died within living memory, so this is a respectful, recognisable portrait taken from the photographs of him at these Games, at this documented moment, with nothing invented. No flags or party emblems of any kind are shown. Correct anatomy, two arms, two legs. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only stadium rim and sky there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    // Arts
    enheduanna: { cap: 'Ur, 23rd century BC', ll: [46.1, 30.96], tx: 'Inspiring: your other Figures here get +1 Might.', fact: 'Daughter of King Sargon and high priestess of the moon god at Ur, she wrote hymns in her own voice and put her name to them. She is the first author in history whose name we know.', art: 'Enheduanna, high priestess of the moon god at Ur, about 2270 BC, composing a hymn at night, dressed as the carved stone disc that bears her own name shows her: a woman of about thirty-five with a strong profile and long hair falling down her back from under a round cap with a rolled brim, in a long robe of tiered woollen flounces that leaves one shoulder bare. She sits on a low stool on the flat roof of the temple house under a thin crescent moon, a small cushion-shaped tablet of damp clay in her left palm and a cut reed in her right hand, pressed to the clay; her lips are parted as she tries the line aloud. A lamp of oil burns in a shell beside her, and a servant waits with a basket of fresh clay. Below lie the mud-brick city and the palm groves along the river. Seen from the roof beside her, from the waist up, her face right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. Early Mesopotamia: no great stepped tower yet (that was built two centuries later), no winged bulls, no Egyptian dress. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only night sky there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    aeschylus: { cap: 'Athens, c. 525 to c. 456 BC', ll: [23.54, 38.04], tx: 'Legacy of Homer. Steadfast: he has +2 Might when he defends.', fact: 'He gave Greek plays a second actor, so that for the first time two people could argue face to face on the stage. He had fought the Persians at Marathon, and the words on his grave spoke of that and never mentioned his plays.', art: 'Aeschylus in the spring of 472 BC, in the theatre under the Acropolis of Athens, rehearsing his play The Persians: a broad, weathered man of fifty-three with a bald crown, a full grey beard and the scarred forearms of an old soldier, in a plain cloak. He stands on the beaten earth of the dancing floor, lifting a painted linen mask with a tall crown, wide eyes and an open mouth, and settling it over the head of an actor who wears a long robe with sleeves in the Persian fashion; a second actor waits with his own mask in his hand. Behind them three men of the chorus in eastern dress, each different in age and build, practise a slow step, and a piper tunes a double pipe. The audience seats are plain wooden benches on the hillside. Seen from the dancing floor, from the waist up, the playwright\'s face right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. Athens in 472 BC: no stone theatre, no raised stage, no comedy masks. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only hillside and sky there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    libai: { cap: 'Tang China, 701 to 762', ll: [104.74, 31.78], tx: 'Voyager: he can walk to any Place.', fact: 'The best-loved poet of China\'s Tang dynasty, he wandered the country for most of his life, and about a thousand of his poems survive, many of them about friends, mountains, the Moon and wine. Legend says he drowned reaching from a boat to embrace the Moon\'s reflection.', art: 'Li Bai about 744, drinking alone under the Moon, as in the best known of his poems: a man of forty-three with a free, handsome face, a thin beard and bright eyes, in the loose robe of a Tang gentleman with wide sleeves, a black gauze cap pushed back on his head and a straight sword at his belt. He stands among blossoming trees on a terrace on a mountainside, his head tilted back, raising a small wine cup high in his right hand toward the full moon as if inviting it to drink, and his left arm is flung out. The moonlight throws his long shadow across the white stones behind him, so that he, the Moon and the shadow make a party of three. A wine jar stands on a rock. Seen from below the terrace, from the knees up, his face right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture and the Moon high at the centre. Tang China: no boat, no drowning, no Japanese dress. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only blossom branches there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    seishonagon: { cap: 'Kyoto, c. 966 to c. 1025', ll: [135.76, 35.02], tx: 'Scholar: you draw a card when she arrives.', fact: 'A lady at the Japanese court, she filled a notebook with lists of things that delighted or annoyed her, sharp stories about the people around her and what she saw from day to day. It is called The Pillow Book, and people have been reading it for a thousand years.', art: 'Sei Shōnagon about the year 995, on a snowy morning in the empress\'s rooms in Kyoto, in the best-known scene of her Pillow Book: a quick-witted court lady of about thirty with a pale oval face and hair that falls to the floor, in many layers of silk robes whose coloured edges show at the neck and sleeves. The empress has asked what the snow looks like on a mountain in an old Chinese poem, and Sei, instead of answering, has gone to the outer wall and is rolling up a hanging blind of fine bamboo with both hands, so that the white garden outside suddenly floods the dim room with light. Behind her the young empress, half hidden by a curtain stand, laughs, and other ladies look up from a charcoal brazier. Seen from inside the room, Sei from the knees up, her face turned back toward us right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. Heian Japan: no writing desk in this picture, no later kimono with a wide sash, no fan held open. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only dark ceiling there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    ferdowsi: { cap: 'Tus, c. 940 to c. 1020', ll: [59.52, 36.48], tx: 'At the end of a round, if you hold his Place, draw a card.', fact: 'He spent some thirty years writing the Shahnameh, the Book of Kings, which tells the story of Persia from the creation of the world in about fifty thousand rhyming couplets. It is one of the longest poems ever written by one person, and it helped to keep the Persian language alive.', art: 'Ferdowsi about the year 1010, aged seventy, in the orchard of his house at Tus in eastern Persia, setting down the last lines of the Book of Kings: an old country gentleman with a long white beard and a tired, satisfied face, in a long quilted robe with a sash and a white turban, sitting on a carpet under a mulberry tree with a board across his knees. He has just lifted his reed pen from the page and holds it in the air. All around him on the carpet, in an open chest and stacked in a niche of the garden wall, are tied bundles of written pages, the work of thirty years, hundreds of them. A boy pours water from a ewer; a single saddled horse waits at the gate. Composed like a Persian manuscript painting, with flat bright colour and a high horizon. Seen from the carpet, from the waist up, his face right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. Persia about the year 1000: no printed books, no heroes or monsters in the sky. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only leaves there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    rumi: { cap: 'Konya, 1207 to 1273', ll: [32.49, 37.87], tx: 'Inspiring: your other Figures here get +1 Might.', fact: 'A teacher of Islamic law in what is now Turkey, he began pouring out poems of love and longing for God after he met a wandering holy man named Shams. His followers became the Mevlevi order, known for a whirling dance that is a form of prayer.', art: 'Rumi about 1250 in the goldsmiths\' street of the market at Konya, in the story his followers told of how the turning dance began: a scholar of about forty-three with a brown beard and a rapt, lifted face, in a wide-sleeved robe and a large wound turban, who has stopped in the middle of the lane with his eyes closed, his right arm rising and his left arm falling, the skirts of his robe just beginning to swing out as he turns in time to the ringing of hammers. In the open-fronted shop beside him a goldbeater and two apprentices, an old man, a youth and a boy, seated at their anvils, have looked up but keep hammering, sheets of gold leaf shining on the bench. Two passers-by, each different, stop with baskets on their heads. Seen from down the lane, from the knees up, his face right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. Seljuk Anatolia in the thirteenth century: no tall felt cap and white skirt of the later dervishes, no Ottoman dress. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only shop awning and shadow there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    cervantes: { cap: 'Alcalá de Henares, 1547 to 1616', ll: [-3.36, 40.48], tx: 'At the end of a round, if you hold his Place, draw a card.', fact: 'A soldier who lost the use of his left hand in a sea battle, he was later seized by pirates and held for five years in Algiers. He was in his late fifties when he published Don Quixote, the story of an old gentleman who reads too many tales of knights and sets out to be one.', art: 'Miguel de Cervantes about 1600, a travelling tax collector in his early fifties, writing at an inn on the plain of La Mancha, with the face he described himself: an eagle\'s nose, chestnut hair, a smooth high forehead, a beard gone silver and large moustaches. He wears a worn black doublet with a small plain collar. He sits at a rough table by the inn\'s open door, writing with his right hand and holding the paper still with his left, which lies stiff and half closed, crippled by an old wound. He has looked up from the page and out of the door, amused, to where a thin old farmer on a bony horse and a stout man on a donkey are passing along the road in the heat, with a row of white windmills turning on the ridge behind them. A jug, bread, his saddlebags. Seen from inside the dark room, from the waist up, his face right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. Spain about 1600: no wide cartwheel ruff, no knight in full armour. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only dark rafters there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    gentileschi: { cap: 'Rome, 1593 to c. 1656', ll: [12.48, 41.9], tx: 'She has +2 Might when she attacks a stronger Figure.', fact: 'She was painting like a master at seventeen, and became the first woman admitted to the academy of art in Florence. Her pictures are known for strong women from old stories, caught in the middle of the action.', art: 'Artemisia Gentileschi about 1638, at work, in the pose of her own self-portrait as the art of painting: a strong-armed woman of forty-five with a round face flushed with effort and dark hair coming loose from its knot, in a green silk dress with the sleeves pushed up above the elbow and a brown apron, a thin gold chain around her neck from which a tiny mask hangs. She leans steeply in from the right toward a tall canvas whose back is to us, her right arm stretched up and across with a long brush at its tip, her left hand low and holding a palette and more brushes, her eyes on a point of the canvas we cannot see. Hard light from a high window falls on her forehead, forearm and bare neck against a plain brown wall. Seen from close beside the easel, from the waist up, her face right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. Nothing of the painted picture is shown. Italy in the 1630s: no beret, no smock of a later age. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only shadowed wall there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    basho: { cap: 'Edo, 1644 to 1694', ll: [139.8, 35.68], tx: 'Legacy of Li Bai. Voyager: he can walk to any Place.', fact: 'In 1689 he sold his house and set off on foot through the wild north of Japan, a journey of some 2,400 kilometres, writing as he went. His travel diary, The Narrow Road to the Deep North, is full of the three-line poems we call haiku.', art: 'Matsuo Bashō in the summer of 1689, aged forty-five, shooting the rapids of the Mogami River in the far north of Japan, swollen with the early summer rains, as his travel diary tells: a slight, weathered man with a shaved head, in the black robe of a lay monk hitched up at the knees, straw sandals and a wide round hat of woven sedge tied under his chin. He kneels in the middle of a long, narrow flat-bottomed rice boat that is racing bow-down between green cliffs, one hand gripping the side and the other clamping his hat to his head, his mouth open in something between terror and delight. Behind him his companion clings to their bundles, and a boatman stands in the stern leaning his whole weight on a long pole. White water, a thread of waterfall down the cliff, rain. Seen from the bank just ahead of the boat, Bashō from the waist up, his face right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. Edo Japan: no sword, no samurai topknot. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only wet cliff and leaves there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    bach: { cap: 'Leipzig, 1685 to 1750', ll: [12.37, 51.34], tx: 'Inspiring: your other Figures here get +1 Might.', fact: 'At twenty he walked some 400 kilometres to hear a famous organist play, and stayed away about three months longer than his employers had allowed. He wrote more than a thousand pieces of music, and four of his sons became composers too.', art: 'Johann Sebastian Bach in the late autumn of 1705, aged twenty, on his long walk across Germany to hear the old organist of Lübeck: a sturdy, square-faced young man with strong dark brows and his own brown hair tied back, in a long travelling coat, muddy boots and a broad hat, a leather satchel of music across his back and a stout stick in his hand. He has come over the last rise of a flat, wet road at sunset and stopped dead: ahead, across the marshes, stand the brick walls of the city and, above them, the two tall green spires of the great church he has walked three weeks to reach. He stares at them, breathing hard, with the beginning of a grin. A carter going the other way looks back at him. Seen from the road in front of him, from the knees up, his face right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture, the city small in the lower left. Germany in 1705: no white wig, no organ, no sheet music in his hand. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only evening sky there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    wheatley: { cap: 'Boston, c. 1753 to 1784', ll: [-71.06, 42.36], tx: 'Legacy of Homer or Virgil. Scholar.', fact: 'Seized in West Africa at about seven and sold in Boston, she was reading the hardest parts of the Bible in English within sixteen months, and went on to Latin. In 1773 she became the first African American to publish a book of poems, after eighteen leading men of Boston signed a paper saying that she had truly written them.', art: 'Phillis Wheatley about 1772, aged about nineteen, writing late at night in the house in Boston where she was held as a slave, in the pose of the portrait printed in her own book: a slight young Black woman with a thoughtful, delicate face, in a plain dark dress with a white kerchief crossed at the neck, a white apron and a frilled white cap. She sits at a small round table with a sheet of paper before her, a quill in her right hand held just above the page, her left hand raised with one finger against her cheek, looking up and away as the next line comes to her. On the table are an inkstand, one candle and a small thick book lying open. The room is dark and bare behind her. Seen from across the table, from the waist up, her face right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. Boston in the 1770s: no chains, no headwrap of a later period, no fine silk gown. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only darkness there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    equiano: { cap: 'Igboland and London, c. 1745 to 1797', ll: [7.03, 5.88], tx: 'Voyager: he can walk to any Place.', fact: 'By his own account he was kidnapped as a boy from what is now Nigeria. Enslaved as a sailor, he traded on his own account until he had saved enough to buy his freedom, and his life story, published in 1789, helped to turn Britain against the slave trade.', art: 'Olaudah Equiano on 11 July 1766, aged about twenty-one, on the Caribbean island of Montserrat, at the moment he becomes a free man: a young Black sailor with a broad, open face and short hair, in a sailor\'s short blue jacket, checked shirt and wide trousers, standing in a merchant\'s counting room with a sheet of paper, his freedom, held in both hands close to his chest. He has just been handed it; his eyes are shut and his head is thrown back, and he is drawing an enormous breath. On the desk in front of him lies the heap of coins he has paid, forty pounds, and behind the desk his former owner, a Quaker merchant in a plain brown coat and broad hat, is still holding the pen. A ship\'s captain claps Equiano on the shoulder. Through the door, the harbour and a sloop at anchor. Seen from the doorway, from the waist up, his face right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. No chains or whips in the picture, no bare-chested figure. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only wooden wall there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    austen: { cap: 'Hampshire, 1775 to 1817', ll: [-0.99, 51.13], tx: 'Scholar: you draw a card when she arrives.', fact: 'She wrote her six novels of love, money and family on small sheets at a little table in the sitting room, and published them without her name; the title page of the first said only “By a Lady”. They have never been out of print.', art: 'Jane Austen about 1813, aged thirty-seven, writing in the dining parlour of her cottage at Chawton: a slim woman with a round face, bright hazel eyes, a small firm mouth and dark curls showing at the front of a white muslin cap, in a plain high-waisted dress with long sleeves. She sits by the window at a very small round walnut table, hardly bigger than a dinner plate, writing on small sheets of paper with a quill. The door of the room, behind her, has begun to open with a creak, and without turning her head she is sliding a sheet of blotting paper over the page with her left hand, her eyes going sideways toward the door and the corner of her mouth lifting. A work basket, a teapot, a garden beyond the glass. Seen from across the little table, from the waist up, her face right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. England in 1813: no bonnet indoors, no wide Victorian skirts, no typewriter. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only wall there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    sequoyah: { cap: 'Cherokee Nation, c. 1770 to 1843', ll: [-84.25, 35.59], tx: 'At the end of a round, if you hold his Place, draw a card.', fact: 'He could not read any language, yet over about twelve years he worked out 86 signs, one for each syllable of Cherokee. Within a few years most of his people could read and write, and they had their own newspaper.', art: 'Sequoyah in 1821, showing the leaders of the Cherokee Nation that his signs can carry speech, with the help of his young daughter Ayoka: a man of about fifty with a calm, deep-lined face, dressed as the portrait painted of him in 1828 shows him, in a blue coat over a white shirt, a red cloth wound around his head as a turban, a long-stemmed pipe in his hand, sitting on a bench with his lame leg stretched out. He watches without a word as his daughter, a girl of about six in a calico dress, who was sent out of the room while the men told him what to write, stands in the doorway and reads their message aloud from a sheet of paper covered in his characters. Three headmen, each different, in turbans, hunting coats and trade-cloth shirts, lean toward her, one with his hand over his mouth. A log council house, a low fire. Seen from beside the girl, so that Sequoyah faces us from the waist up, his face right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. No feathered headdress, no tipi, no buckskin fringe costume. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only log wall there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    hiroshige: { cap: 'Edo, 1797 to 1858', ll: [139.77, 35.69], tx: 'Legacy of Hokusai.', fact: 'The son of a fire warden, he kept his own post in the Edo fire brigade for years while he learned to draw. His prints of travellers caught in rain, snow and mist on the road to Kyoto sold in the thousands.', art: 'Hiroshige in 1832, aged thirty-five, sketching on the great road from Edo to Kyoto as a sudden summer storm breaks: a round-faced, mild-looking man with a shaved crown and a small topknot, in a travelling kimono tucked up at the knees with leggings and straw sandals, sitting on the bench of a roadside tea stall under its dripping thatched eave. A small sketchbook is open on his knee and his brush is moving fast, but his eyes are on the road, where two bearers with a covered litter and a man hunched under a paper umbrella are running bent double up a hill into slanting grey rain, while a grove of bamboo behind them bows in the wind in three fading bands of grey. The tea woman holds out a cup he has not noticed. Seen from the road, looking in under the eave, Hiroshige from the waist up, his face right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. Edo Japan: no great wave, no Mount Fuji, no samurai armour. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only thatch and rain there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    goethe: { cap: 'Weimar, 1749 to 1832', ll: [11.33, 50.98], tx: 'Legacy of Shakespeare. Scholar.', fact: 'Poet, playwright, minister of a small German state and student of plants and colours, he worked on his great play Faust, about a scholar who bargains with the devil, for some sixty years. He finished it only months before he died.', art: 'Johann Wolfgang von Goethe in April 1787, aged thirty-seven, in the public garden at Palermo in Sicily, where the idea came to him that all plants are variations on one plan: a tall, handsome man with large dark eyes, a strong nose and his brown hair tied back unpowdered, in a pale travelling coat, with a broad-brimmed grey hat lying on the path beside him. He has gone down on one knee in front of a bed of strange southern plants and holds a single large leaf flat on his palm, tracing its ribs with a pencil, while a pocket volume of Homer, which he came out to read, lies forgotten face down in the grass. Lemon trees, palms and tall flowering stems surround him, and the sea is blue beyond a wall. Seen from the flower bed, low, from the waist up, his face right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. Italy in 1787: no powdered wig, no devil or scholar\'s study, no reclining pose among ruins. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only palm fronds there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    dickinson: { cap: 'Amherst, 1830 to 1886', ll: [-72.52, 42.38], tx: 'She can\'t be clashed.', fact: 'She seldom left her family\'s house, and in later years spoke to visitors from behind a half-open door. Only about ten of her poems were printed while she lived; after she died her sister found nearly eighteen hundred, many sewn by hand into little books.', art: 'Emily Dickinson about 1865, aged thirty-five, at the upstairs window of her family\'s brick house in Amherst, lowering a treat to the children of the neighbourhood: a small, slight woman with auburn hair parted in the middle and drawn back, dark eyes and a quick, shy smile, in a plain white cotton dress with a row of buttons down the front. She leans out over the sill with both hands paying out a cord, at the end of which a little basket of gingerbread swings down past the shutters toward three children who stand in the garden below with their arms stretched up, only the tops of their heads and their hands in the picture. Behind her in the room is a small square writing table with a lamp and a few folded papers. Summer leaves frame the window. Seen from outside and a little below, Emily from the waist up in the window, her face right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. New England in the 1860s: no black dress, no veil, no gravestones. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only brick wall and leaves there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    tolstoy: { cap: 'Yasnaya Polyana, 1828 to 1910', ll: [37.52, 54.07], tx: 'Inspiring: your other Figures here get +1 Might.', fact: 'A count who had fought as an army officer, he wrote War and Peace, a novel with more than five hundred characters. In old age he dressed as a peasant, worked in the fields and taught that nobody should answer violence with violence.', art: 'Leo Tolstoy about 1890, aged sixty-two, mowing hay with the peasants on his own estate: a big, strong old man with a long grey-white beard, shaggy brows and small piercing eyes, in a loose white peasant blouse belted at the waist, rough trousers tucked into boots and a soft cap. He swings a long-handled scythe between two other mowers in a meadow at sunrise, the grass falling in a curved swath at his feet, sweat darkening his shirt, his face set and content. The two men are younger than he is and different from each other, one fair and broad, one dark and thin, and one glances at him to see whether the old count can keep the pace. One woman rakes behind; there is no line of repeated mowers; a white manor house shows through birch trees far off. Seen from in front of the line, low, Tolstoy from the knees up, his face right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. Russia in the 1890s: no officer\'s uniform, no writing desk, no fur coat. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only dawn sky there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    tchaikovsky: { cap: 'St Petersburg, 1840 to 1893', ll: [30.3, 59.93], tx: 'Legacy of Mozart.', fact: 'He trained as a law clerk before he gave himself to music. His ballets Swan Lake, The Sleeping Beauty and The Nutcracker are danced all over the world, though Swan Lake was coldly received when it was first staged.', art: 'Pyotr Tchaikovsky in February 1877, aged thirty-six, at a rehearsal of his first ballet in the great theatre in Moscow: a neat, anxious man with a short dark beard, a high forehead and gentle eyes, in a dark frock coat, sitting alone in the empty front rows of the stalls with the big handwritten score open across his knees and a pencil between his teeth, both hands gripping the seat in front of him. On the stage above, in the hard light of the gas footlights, four dancers in white knee-length skirts of gauze with small wings at the shoulders, each different in height and pose, wait against a painted lake and a painted moon, and the ballet master is waving his stick at the four of them in a temper. The gilded tiers of boxes rise empty into the dark. Seen from the side aisle, so that the lit stage is at the left and he sits at the right, Tchaikovsky from the waist up, his head right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. Russia in 1877: no short stiff modern tutu, no grey-haired old man. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only dark boxes there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    twain: { cap: 'Hannibal, Missouri, 1835 to 1910', ll: [-91.36, 39.71], tx: 'On arrival, gain 1 Spark, or 2 if the other player holds this Place.', fact: 'Samuel Clemens took his pen name from the cry of the Mississippi riverboat men, “mark twain”, which means water two fathoms deep and safe to steam through. He was born in a year when Halley\'s Comet passed, said he expected to go out with it, and died the day after it came closest to the Sun again.', art: 'Samuel Clemens, later Mark Twain, in 1859, aged twenty-three, a newly licensed pilot on a Mississippi steamboat: a slim young man with a thick mop of reddish-brown curls, bushy brows over sharp grey-green eyes and side whiskers, in a blue pilot\'s jacket with brass buttons over a white shirt. He stands high in the glass-sided pilot house at an enormous wooden steering wheel, taller than he is and half sunk through the floor, with both hands on its spokes and his whole body leaning into the turn, his eyes narrowed on the brown river ahead where a line of ripples marks a sandbar at a bend. A speaking tube and a bell pull hang beside him, and the tall black smokestacks of the boat rise outside the glass. Far below on the foredeck a man is swinging a sounding line. Dawn mist on the water. Seen from the back of the pilot house, a little to one side, from the knees up, his face in three-quarter view right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. No white suit, no white hair or moustache, no cigar. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only window frame and mist there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    vangogh: { cap: 'Arles, 1853 to 1890', ll: [4.63, 43.68], tx: 'Legacy of Hiroshige. Swift: he can walk on the turn he arrives.', fact: 'He took up painting at twenty-seven and made nearly nine hundred paintings in the ten years he had left, at times one a day. He sold very few, and never knew that they would be among the best loved in the world.', art: 'Vincent van Gogh in September 1888, aged thirty-five, painting the stars over the river Rhône at Arles, out of doors at night, as he wrote that he did: a wiry man with cropped red hair, a red beard and pale, burning eyes, in a blue workman\'s jacket and a battered felt hat, standing on the stone quay at a folding easel set under a street gas lamp. He holds a loaded brush out at arm\'s length toward the sky, measuring, and a palette thick with paint is hooked on his other thumb. Beyond him the dark river carries long trembling reflections of the town\'s yellow gaslights, and overhead the stars of the Great Bear hang large in a deep blue-green sky. A couple strolls on the bank below. Seen from along the quay, from the knees up, his face lit from above right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. No candles on his hat, no bandaged ear, no sunflowers, and his canvas is turned away so that the painting itself is not shown. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only night sky there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    joplin: { cap: 'Sedalia, Missouri, c. 1868 to 1917', ll: [-93.23, 38.7], tx: 'Swift: he can walk on the turn he arrives.', fact: 'His Maple Leaf Rag of 1899 sold hundreds of thousands of copies as sheet music and paid him a penny on every one. He was called the King of Ragtime, and more than seventy years later his tune The Entertainer was a hit all over again.', art: 'Scott Joplin in the late summer of 1899, aged about thirty-one, in a music shop in Sedalia, Missouri, on the day his Maple Leaf Rag is first in print: a slight, quiet, serious young Black man with close-cut hair and a clean-shaven face, in a neat dark three-piece suit with a high white collar and a tie, standing at the shop counter. He has taken the top copy from a fresh stack of sheet music and holds it open in both hands, reading down the page, his lips pressed together and the fingers of his right hand, without his knowing it, tapping the rhythm on the edge of the paper. The white-bearded shop owner watches him over his spectacles from behind the counter. Violins and banjos hang on the wall and an upright piano stands closed in the corner. Seen from the shop door, from the waist up, his face right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. Missouri in 1899: nobody is playing, no straw boater and striped jacket, no saloon. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only shelves in shadow there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    kahlo: { cap: 'Coyoacán, Mexico, 1907 to 1954', ll: [-99.16, 19.35], tx: 'Steadfast: she has +2 Might when she defends.', fact: 'A bus crash at eighteen broke her spine and left her in pain for the rest of her life. She began to paint lying in bed, with a mirror fixed above her, and about a third of all her pictures are portraits of herself.', art: 'Frida Kahlo in 1926, aged nineteen, a year after the accident, painting in bed at her family\'s blue house in Coyoacán: a young woman with strong dark brows that almost meet, black hair parted in the middle and pinned back, and a level, unflinching gaze, propped on pillows in a wooden four-poster bed. Her body is held rigid from hips to armpits in a white plaster corset under an embroidered blouse. A small wooden easel made for her stands across her lap with a canvas on it, its back to us, and she holds a fine brush in her right hand and a little palette in her left, but she is not looking at the canvas: she is looking up, into a mirror fixed to the underside of the bed\'s canopy, in which we see her face again, small and upside down. Paint tubes on the blanket, a window on a courtyard with a lemon tree. Seen from the foot of the bed, from the waist up, her face right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture and the mirror above her at the top centre. She died within living memory, so this is a respectful, recognisable portrait taken from the photographs of her at this age, at this documented moment, with nothing invented. No monkey, no flower crown, no skeletons. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only canopy cloth there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    // Wonder
    tutankhamun: { cap: 'Thebes, ruled c. 1332 to 1323 BC', ll: [32.6, 25.74], tx: 'He can\'t be clashed.', fact: 'He became pharaoh at about nine and died at about nineteen. His small tomb lay hidden under the huts of later tomb builders for more than three thousand years, and when it was opened in 1922 it still held over five thousand objects, among them a mask of solid gold.', art: 'Tutankhamun about 1325 BC, aged about seventeen, in a palace garden with his young queen, drawn after the scenes on the furniture from his own tomb: a slender youth with a narrow face and large almond eyes, in a long pleated kilt of fine white linen with a wide sash, a broad collar of coloured beads on his bare chest, sandals, and a close blue crown with a gold cobra at the brow. He leans with both hands on a tall walking stick, one foot set a little behind the other, as scenes from his tomb show him, and bends his head toward the queen, who stands facing him in a clinging pleated dress and a heavy wig and holds up to him two bunches of lotus and papyrus flowers. Behind them are a pool with ducks, fruit trees and a painted pavilion. Composed in the soft, natural style of the art of his own short reign. Seen from the side, from the knees up, his face right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. No gold mask on his face, no mummy, no curse. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only leaves and sky there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    ramesses: { cap: 'Egypt, ruled 1279 to 1213 BC', ll: [31.63, 22.34], tx: 'He can\'t be moved. He has +1 Might for each round he stays, up to +2.', fact: 'He ruled Egypt for sixty-six years, fathered about a hundred children and built on a grander scale than any pharaoh before or after. At Abu Simbel he had a temple cut into a cliff so that twice a year the rising Sun reaches the statues at its very back.', art: 'Ramesses II about 1250 BC, in his fifties, inside his rock temple at Abu Simbel at the moment of sunrise on one of the two days of the year when the light reaches its heart: a tall, lean pharaoh with a high-bridged, hooked nose and a long jaw, in a pleated linen robe with wide sleeves, a broad jewelled collar and the tall blue war crown with a gold cobra, holding up a small bowl of burning incense in both hands. He stands in the dark inner chamber, and a single level beam of gold light, come all the way down the long hall behind us, strikes his raised arms and face and the knees of four seated stone figures against the back wall behind him, of which three are lit and the one at the left end stays in shadow. Painted reliefs glimmer on the side walls. Seen from the hall, looking in along the beam, the king from the knees up, his face right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. No pyramids, no striped headcloth, no mummy. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only darkness there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    pythagoras: { cap: 'Samos and Croton, c. 570 to c. 495 BC', ll: [26.94, 37.69], tx: 'Inspiring: your other Figures here get +1 Might.', fact: 'He led a secretive brotherhood in southern Italy who lived by strict rules, kept away from meat, and believed that number is the key to everything. They found that strings whose lengths stand as two to one, or three to two, sound sweet together.', art: 'Pythagoras about 520 BC at Croton in southern Italy, showing his followers that music is number: a tall, grave man of about fifty with long hair and a full dark beard streaked with grey, in a long white linen robe, standing in a courtyard at dusk. He holds across his body a plain sounding box of wood with a single gut string stretched along it, and with one finger he presses a small movable bridge exactly at the middle of the string while his other hand plucks one half; his head is bent to the sound. Five followers, men and women of different ages, all in white, sit on the ground before him in silence, two with their eyes closed, and one young man holds a knotted cord marked off in equal parts. The first stars are out above the wall. Seen from among the listeners, Pythagoras from the knees up, his face right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. Greek Italy in the sixth century BC: no triangle diagram held up like a sign, no telescope, no lyre with many strings. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only dusk sky there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    pakal: { cap: 'Palenque, 603 to 683', ll: [-92.05, 17.48], tx: 'He can\'t be moved. He has +1 Might for each round he stays, up to +2.', fact: 'He became king of the Maya city of Palenque at twelve and ruled for sixty-eight years, one of the longest reigns in history. In 1952 his tomb was found deep inside a pyramid, his face covered with a mask of jade.', art: 'Pakal of Palenque on the day in the year 615 when he becomes king at twelve years old, drawn after the carved stone panel in his own palace that shows the scene: a slim boy with the long sloping forehead and high-bridged nose the Maya admired, his hair cut in steps and tied up in a plume, wearing a short kilt, a wide collar and wristbands of green jade beads. He sits cross-legged on a low throne carved as a jaguar with a head at each end, turned toward his mother, who kneels before him in a net-patterned dress and cape and lifts up to him in both hands a tall crown made of small plates of jade and shell. He reaches for it gravely with one hand. Behind them are the white plastered walls of a palace room painted with red bands, and through a doorway the roof combs of temples stand above the forest in mist. Seen from the side, Pakal from the waist up, his face right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. The Maya of the seventh century: no Aztec sun stone, no feather war bonnet, no sacrifice. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only plaster wall there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    hildegard: { cap: 'Rhineland, 1098 to 1179', ll: [7.9, 49.97], tx: 'Scholar: you draw a card when she arrives.', fact: 'An abbess on the Rhine, she wrote down the visions she had seen since childhood, along with books about medicine and plants. More of her music survives than of any other composer of her time, some seventy songs with their words and their tunes.', art: 'Hildegard of Bingen about 1150, aged fifty-two, receiving a vision and writing it down, drawn after the picture at the front of her own book: a woman with a pale, strong face lifted upward and wide grey eyes, in the black habit and veil of a Benedictine nun, seated on a wooden chair in a small arched cell. From a square opening in the roof above her five long tongues of red and gold flame reach down and touch her forehead, and she, without looking at her hands, is writing with a pointed stylus on a pair of hinged wax tablets held on her knee. At the right edge a tonsured monk, her secretary, has put his head through a little window in the cell wall and stares up at the flames, a sheet of parchment forgotten in his hand. Composed with the flat colour and gold ground of a twelfth-century manuscript painting. Seen from inside the cell, from the waist up, her face right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. No halo, no harp, no witch\'s herbs. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only painted arch there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    lalibela: { cap: 'Roha, Ethiopia, ruled c. 1181 to 1221', ll: [39.04, 12.03], tx: 'He can\'t be moved. He has +1 Might for each round he stays, up to +2.', fact: 'King of Ethiopia, he is remembered for eleven churches at his capital that were not built up from the ground but carved down into it, each cut from the solid rock. Pilgrims still come to pray in them, and the town now bears his name.', art: 'Lalibela, king of Ethiopia, about the year 1200, at the rim of the pit in which one of his churches is being carved downward out of the living rock: a lean, dark, bearded man of middle age with a calm, absorbed face, in long white cotton robes with a coloured border, a white cloth wound around his head beneath a plain gold band, holding a tall staff topped with a cross. He stands at the very edge of a deep square trench cut in a hillside of red-brown stone and looks down into it. Below him, at the bottom of the picture, the flat roof of the church is already finished, a great cross within a cross carved in the rock, level with the ground, and far down around its walls three tiny masons, each doing something different, are at work: one on a ladder hollowing out a window with an iron pick, one cutting a doorway, one carrying away a basket of chips. Two priests in white stand behind the king, one with a drum and one with a book. Seen from across the trench, the king from the knees up, his face right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. No angels shown, no European crown, no built stonework of blocks. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only dry hillside and sky there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    grace: { cap: 'Clew Bay, Ireland, c. 1530 to c. 1603', ll: [-9.75, 53.83], tx: 'Voyager. Raider: she has +2 Might when she attacks on the other player\'s side.', fact: 'Chieftain of a seafaring clan on the west coast of Ireland, she commanded her own fleet of galleys for decades, and the English called her a pirate. At about sixty-three she sailed to London and argued her case before Queen Elizabeth, who granted most of what she asked.', art: 'Grace O\'Malley, Gráinne Ní Mháille, about 1577, at her tower house on the shore of Clew Bay in the west of Ireland, sending her galleys to sea: a tall, weathered woman in her late forties with grey-streaked dark hair blowing loose and a hard, humorous face, in the dress of a Gaelic noblewoman, a full-sleeved linen tunic dyed yellow under a heavy fringed wool mantle fastened at the breast, a long knife at her belt. She stands at the head of the sea steps under a plain square stone tower, one hand on the wet rock, the other pointing out to the mouth of the bay, calling an order down to the nearer of two long, low ships with a single mast and a row of oars on each side, whose crew is pushing off; four of them can be seen, each different. A mooring rope runs from the ship up through a slit window of the tower above her. Islands, rain cloud and a gap of bright sea. Seen from the water below the steps, from the knees up, her face right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. Sixteenth-century Ireland: no eyepatch, no three-cornered hat, no skull flag, no cannon broadside. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only tower wall and cloud there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    shahjahan: { cap: 'Agra, 1592 to 1666', ll: [78.04, 27.17], tx: 'On arrival, gain 2 Spark.', fact: 'When his wife Mumtaz Mahal died in 1631, the emperor of India set some twenty thousand workers to build her a tomb of white marble, the Taj Mahal. He spent his last eight years a prisoner of his own son, in a fort from which he could see it across the river.', art: 'Shah Jahan about 1665, in his seventies, a prisoner in the fort at Agra, looking across the river at the tomb he built for his wife: a thin, upright old man with a long white beard and a fine, sad face, in a plain white muslin coat tied at the side and a small turban with a single pearl, seated on a cushion in an eight-sided marble pavilion whose walls are inlaid with flowers of coloured stone. He sits turned away from the room, one hand on the carved marble screen of the balcony, gazing out. Beyond the screen the wide river bends away in the haze of early morning, and on its far bank, small and pale and perfect, stand the white dome and four slim towers of the Taj Mahal. His eldest daughter sits behind him with a book. Seen from inside the pavilion, a little to one side, the emperor from the waist up, his face in profile right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture, the tomb in the distance at the centre left. Mughal India: no jewelled throne, no courtiers, no sword. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only inlaid marble wall there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    shelley: { cap: 'London, 1797 to 1851', ll: [-0.13, 51.53], tx: 'Legacy of Mary Wollstonecraft. Scholar.', fact: 'Kept indoors by storms at a house on Lake Geneva in the cold summer of 1816, she and her friends agreed that each would write a ghost story. She was eighteen, and hers became Frankenstein, the tale of a student who builds a living man and runs from what he has made.', art: 'Mary Godwin, soon to be Mary Shelley, in June 1816, aged eighteen, on the stormy night at a villa above Lake Geneva when the idea of Frankenstein comes to her: a slight, fair girl with a high clear forehead, hazel eyes and light brown hair parted in the middle, in a plain high-waisted dress with a shawl. She stands alone at a tall window with one hand pressed flat to the glass and a candle in the other, and she has gone rigid, staring not at the lake but at something in her own mind; a fork of lightning over the black water and the far mountains lights her face white. Behind her in the room three young men talk by the fire, careless shapes in firelight with books and wine glasses, and none of them has noticed her. Rain on the panes. Seen from outside the window looking in, Mary from the waist up, her face right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. 1816: no monster shown anywhere, no laboratory, no bolts, no castle. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only dark window frame and rain there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    andersen: { cap: 'Odense, 1805 to 1875', ll: [10.39, 55.4], tx: 'At the end of a round, if you hold his Place, draw a card.', fact: 'A poor shoemaker\'s son, he left home alone at fourteen to seek his fortune in Copenhagen. He wrote more than 150 fairy tales, among them The Little Mermaid, The Ugly Duckling and The Emperor\'s New Clothes, and he could cut whole scenes out of folded paper while he told them.', art: 'Hans Christian Andersen about 1850, aged forty-five, telling a story to the children of a Danish country house and cutting it out of paper as he goes: a very tall, thin, awkward man with a long nose, deep-set small eyes and a kind, mobile face, in a black frock coat with a high collar and a loose silk tie, perched on a chair too low for him with his knees up. In his big hands are a pair of enormous paper scissors and a folded sheet of white paper, from which he is just drawing apart, like a concertina, a long chain of joined figures: a row of dancers, a swan at each end, a little man in a tall hat. Four children press around his knees, one already holding an earlier cutting up to the window so that the daylight shines through it. A tiled stove, a rag rug. Seen from behind the smallest child, Andersen from the waist up, his face right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. Denmark about 1850: no mermaid or duckling drawn in the room, no wizard\'s robe. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only wall there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    poe: { cap: 'Baltimore, 1809 to 1849', ll: [-76.62, 39.29], tx: 'On arrival, the strongest enemy Figure here misses its next turn.', fact: 'He wrote poems and tales of dread, among them The Raven, and in 1841 he invented the detective story, with a hero who solves a murder by reasoning alone. He was found dying in Baltimore at forty, and nobody has ever explained what had happened to him.', art: 'Edgar Allan Poe in the winter of 1846, aged thirty-seven, writing by night in his small cottage outside New York: a slight man with a very broad pale forehead, dark tumbled hair, a dark moustache and large, shadowed grey eyes, in a worn black coat buttoned to the neck against the cold. He sits at a bare table writing with a steel pen on a long narrow strip of paper made of sheets pasted end to end, which trails off the table and curls on the floor. A large tortoiseshell cat sits upright on his shoulder, as she really did, watching the pen. One candle; its light throws his shadow and the cat\'s, joined into a single strange tall shape, up the whitewashed wall behind him. A cold hearth and a few books on a hanging shelf. Seen from across the table, from the waist up, his face right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. No raven, no skull, no bottle. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only bare wall above the shadow there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    verne: { cap: 'Nantes and Amiens, 1828 to 1905', ll: [2.3, 49.89], tx: 'Legacy of Edgar Allan Poe. Voyager: he can walk to any Place.', fact: 'Before a submarine had crossed an ocean or anyone had left the ground in an aeroplane, he sent his heroes under the sea, around the Moon and around the world in eighty days, working out each journey with the science and the timetables of his day. He is one of the most translated authors there has ever been.', art: 'Jules Verne about 1870, aged forty-two, writing in the tiny cabin of his own sailing boat on the coast of northern France, where he wrote much of his tale of a voyage under the sea: a broad-shouldered man with curly brown hair, a full beard and lively pale eyes, in a seaman\'s knitted jersey and a pea jacket, sitting wedged on a narrow bunk at a fold-down table that slopes with the boat. He writes in pencil in a thick notebook, steadying an inkwell with his elbow, and has looked up at the round brass-rimmed window beside his head, against which a green wave has just broken, so that for a moment the glass is full of water and light, as if the boat were under the sea. A sea chart, a lamp swinging on its hook, a row of scientific journals behind a rail. Seen from the cabin door, from the waist up, his face right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. France about 1870: no submarine, no diving helmet, no balloon. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only dark deck beams there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    oakley: { cap: 'Darke County, Ohio, 1860 to 1926', ll: [-84.63, 40.25], tx: 'She has +2 Might when she attacks a stronger Figure.', fact: 'She learned to shoot as a child to feed her family, and at fifteen she out-shot a travelling marksman, whom she later married. In Buffalo Bill\'s show she could hit the thin edge of a playing card at thirty paces.', art: 'Annie Oakley about 1888, aged twenty-eight, in the arena of the Wild West show, making her most famous shot: a very small, neat woman with long dark hair down her back and a calm, intent face, in the costume she sewed herself, a calf-length skirt and a fitted jacket with a line of fringe, leggings, and a wide-brimmed hat pinned up at one side with a star, a row of shooting medals on her chest. She stands with her back to her target, a rifle laid over her right shoulder pointing behind her, and aims by looking into a small hand mirror that she holds up in her left hand; in the mirror we can just see the tiny reflection of a man far across the arena holding out a playing card edge-on. Her face is all concentration, one eye closed. Canvas tents, and the grandstand only a soft blur of colour with no faces drawn. Seen from in front of her, from the waist up, her face right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. No feathers, no short skirt, no two pistols twirling. Correct anatomy, two hands. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only tent canvas and sky there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    houdini: { cap: 'New York, 1874 to 1926', ll: [-73.95, 40.8], tx: 'Swift. He can\'t be clashed.', fact: 'Born Erik Weisz in Budapest, he could slip out of handcuffs, chains, jail cells and a locked tank of water in which he hung upside down. He also spent years exposing fake mediums who claimed to speak with the dead.', art: 'Harry Houdini about 1916, aged forty-two, hanging head down in a straitjacket high above a city street: a short, very muscular man with curly dark hair standing out from his head and a face clenched with effort, his ankles bound in a padded harness to a rope that runs up to a beam jutting from the top of a tall newspaper building. He is strapped into a canvas straitjacket with leather buckles, his arms crossed and pinned, and he is doubled up at the waist, twisting, with one shoulder already worked half free. He fills the upper part of the picture, upside down, and far beneath him the street is a soft blur of straw hats and stopped trams, with no faces drawn. At one near window four people lean out to watch, each different: a clerk in shirtsleeves, a woman with her hand to her mouth, a newsboy and an old man. Seen from a window level with him, his whole figure in the upper half, hanging right of centre, his face turned toward us enough to read (he hangs head down, so his feet, not his head, are nearest the top). He is drawn from the photographs and films of these escapes, respectfully and recognisably, with nothing invented. No chains, no water tank, no top hat or wand. Correct anatomy. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only sky and the edge of the building there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    tenzing: { cap: 'Khumbu, Nepal, 1914 to 1986', ll: [86.93, 27.99], tx: 'Voyager: he can walk to any Place.', fact: 'A Sherpa who herded yaks as a boy in sight of Mount Everest, he first went to the mountain as a young porter in 1935 and went back six more times. In 1952 he climbed higher on it than anyone had before, and the year after that he stood on the top.', art: 'Tenzing Norgay on 30 May 1953, aged thirty-nine, at the camp in the high valley below Mount Everest, the day after he stood on its summit, as the photographs taken that day show him: a lean, strong Sherpa with a wide, brilliant smile, deep creases at the corners of his eyes, a sun-darkened face with paler skin where his goggles sat, and thick black hair blown about, in a quilted down jacket open at the neck over a wool jersey, with a knotted scarf. He sits on a wooden ration box holding an enamel mug of tea in both bare hands, laughing at someone out of the picture. His ice axe is stuck upright in the snow beside him and his goggles hang at his chest. Behind him are one small orange tent, a coil of rope, and a wall of blue-white ice and snow rising out of the picture. He is the only person shown. Seen from close in front, from the waist up, his head right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. He died within living memory, so this is a respectful, recognisable portrait taken from the photographs of him in 1953, at this documented moment, with nothing invented. 1953 clothing and gear: no modern bright climbing suit, no helmet, no flags in this picture, no oxygen mask on his face. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only snow slope and deep blue sky there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    // Relics
    astrolabe: { cap: 'Alexandria and Baghdad, from the 4th century', ll: [29.9, 31.2], tx: 'Your Figures can walk two steps.', fact: 'With one brass disc a traveller could tell the time by the Sun or a star, find the direction of Mecca and measure the height of a tower. An astronomer of the tenth century described about a thousand uses for an astrolabe.', art: 'A brass astrolabe of the Islamic world, about the tenth century, hanging from a hand by the ring at its top against a clear night sky: a flat round instrument a little wider than the hand that holds it, its face an openwork plate of curling pointers, each one marking a star, laid over a disc engraved with fine arcs and circles, with a straight sighting bar pinned across the back. It turns slightly on its ring and catches lamplight. Behind it, out of focus, are the flat roofs and one dome of a city and a field of real stars that the pointers echo. Only the hand and wrist of the holder are in the picture, in a plain wide sleeve. Historically accurate: no glass, no magnetic needle, no numbers or letters that can be read. The instrument is in the upper half, right of centre. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain dark sky.' },
    paper: { cap: 'China, from the 2nd century', ll: [112.45, 34.62], tx: 'Draw one more card each turn.', fact: 'For centuries only China knew how to make paper. It reached Samarkand in the 700s and Baghdad before the year 800, and Spain about two hundred years after that, and wherever it arrived, books became cheaper.', art: 'Newly made sheets of paper drying in the sun in a Chinese papermaker\'s yard of about the second century: a long whitewashed wall with a dozen pale, slightly rough-edged sheets pressed flat against it, the nearest one beginning to curl away at one corner in the breeze, its fibres catching the light. In front of the wall stand a wooden vat of grey pulp with a cloth-covered frame resting across it, a stone mortar, and a basket of bark strips, rags and a tangle of old fishing net. A single finished sheet lies on a low table under a brush and an ink stone, still blank. No people. Historically accurate: no printed or written characters anywhere, no bound books, no scrolls on rollers. The drying sheets fill the upper half. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain wall and sky.' },
    rosetta: { cap: 'Egypt, 196 BC', ll: [30.42, 31.4], tx: 'Draw one more card each turn.', fact: 'The same decree is carved on the stone three times: in hieroglyphs, in the everyday script of Egypt and in Greek. Scholars could read the Greek, and that was their way in to the other two.', art: 'The Rosetta Stone as it was found in July 1799, half dug out of the rubble of an old fort wall in the Nile delta: a slab of dark grey stone about as tall as a child of ten, its top corners and one lower corner broken away, standing tilted in a trench of sand and broken brick. Its polished face is covered from top to bottom with fine carved writing in three bands that are plainly different from each other: small pictures at the top, a flowing script in the middle, and neat square letters at the bottom. A soldier\'s pick and a wooden measuring rod lean beside it, and a hand has just brushed the dust from the middle band. Hard sunlight rakes across the carving. No people but the one hand. Historically accurate: no writing that can be read, no gold, no pyramids behind. The stone stands in the upper half, right of centre. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain sky and wall.' },
    locomotive: { cap: 'England, 1829', ll: [-2.76, 53.42], tx: 'Your Figures can walk two steps.', fact: 'At trials near Liverpool in 1829 a locomotive named Rocket ran at about thirty miles an hour, a speed no horse could keep up for long. Within twenty years Britain had laid some six thousand miles of railway.', art: 'The locomotive Rocket at speed in October 1829: a small engine painted bright yellow with black bands, with a tall thin white chimney at the front, a short barrel of a boiler, two big spoked driving wheels turned by cylinders set at a slant on each side, and a pair of small wheels behind, pulling a four-wheeled tender that carries a water barrel. It rushes from left to right along a plain iron track on a low embankment, white steam streaming back flat from the chimney, the driver and fireman small dark figures on the footplate, one holding his hat on. A marquee with two flags stands far back; nobody else is in the picture. Seen from the trackside, low, the engine in the upper half, right of centre. Historically accurate: no cab roof, no cowcatcher, no headlamp, no later streamlining. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain sky and steam.' },
    balloon: { cap: 'Paris, 1783', ll: [2.26, 48.86], tx: 'Once a turn, jump one of your Figures to any Place. It is done for the turn.', fact: 'The first passengers in a hot-air balloon, sent up before the king of France in 1783, were a sheep, a duck and a rooster. Two months later two men flew across Paris for about twenty-five minutes, feeding a fire of straw beneath the paper-lined bag as they went.', art: 'The balloon of the Montgolfier brothers on 21 November 1783, floating over the roofs of Paris with the first two people ever to fly: a huge pear-shaped bag of cloth and paper painted deep blue and gold, decorated with suns, swags of drapery and the signs of the zodiac, taller than a church. Around its open neck runs a narrow wicker gallery, and in it, on opposite sides to balance it, stand two tiny men in coats and hats, one waving his hat and the other pushing a forkful of straw into an iron fire basket that hangs in the opening. A thread of smoke trails away. Below lie grey slate roofs, church towers and the river with its bridges, and on one near roof three people, each different, looking up. Seen from the air a little below the balloon, which fills the upper half, right of centre. Historically accurate: no gas burner, no hanging basket on ropes, no modern bright stripes. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain clear sky.' },
    wrightflyer: { cap: 'Kitty Hawk, 1903', ll: [-75.67, 36.02], tx: 'Once a turn, jump one of your Figures to any Place. It is done for the turn.', fact: 'On 17 December 1903 two bicycle makers from Ohio, Orville and Wilbur Wright, made the first flights in a powered aeroplane. The first lasted twelve seconds and covered less ground than the wingspan of a modern airliner.', art: 'The Wright brothers\' aeroplane on the morning of 17 December 1903 at the instant it leaves the ground: a frail machine of spruce and pale cloth with two long wings one above the other, a pair of small flat surfaces held out in front on struts, a double rudder behind, and two slow wooden propellers at the back driven by chains. It has just lifted a hand\'s breadth off the end of a single wooden rail laid on flat sand. One man lies face down on the lower wing with his hips in a cradle and his hands on a lever; the other, in a dark suit and cap, has let go of the wing tip and stands frozen in mid-stride, watching. A low bench and a coil of wire lie on the sand. Cold grey light, wind off the sea, a big dune behind. Seen from behind and to the right, the machine in the upper half, right of centre. Historically accurate: no wheels, no seat, no enclosed body, no engine cowling. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain blank winter sky.' },
    stirrup: { cap: 'North China, 4th and 5th centuries', ll: [120.77, 41.8], tx: 'Your Figures can walk two steps.', fact: 'The oldest pair of stirrups that can be dated lay in the tomb of a nobleman buried in the north of China in 415. With a foot braced on each side a rider could stay in the saddle all day and stand up in it to fight.', art: 'One early stirrup of north China, about the year 415, seen very close and large from the side as it hangs against the flank of a standing horse: a rounded loop a little wider than a foot, with a flat tread at the bottom and a long straight handle rising from its top, made of bent wood covered in thin gilded bronze held by small rivets, hung by a plain leather strap from a wooden saddle with a high bow in front and behind. A rider\'s soft felt boot rests in the loop; only the leg below the knee is in the picture. The horse is a short, stocky steppe horse with a thick mane, and the second stirrup shows under its belly on the far side. Dry grass and low hills behind, evening light on the gilding. Historically accurate: no spurs, no horseshoes, no iron plate armour on the horse. The stirrup is right of centre in the upper half. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain sky.' },
    sextant: { cap: 'London, 1757', ll: [-0.12, 51.5], tx: 'Once a turn, jump one of your Figures to any Place. It is done for the turn.', fact: 'A sextant measures how high the Sun or a star stands above the horizon, and from that a navigator can work out how far north or south the ship is. Its curved scale is one sixth of a circle, which gave it its name. The first one was made in London in 1757.', art: 'A brass sextant of about 1760 held up to the eye on the deck of a sailing ship, seen close from the side: a flat frame shaped like a slice of pie, about as wide as a forearm is long, with a curved scale along its lower edge marked in fine lines, a small telescope along the top, two little mirrors, and a swinging arm with a clamp that the navigator\'s right thumb is turning. His left hand steadies the frame by its wooden handle. Only the hands, the cuffs of a dark blue coat and part of a cheek and a tricorn hat are in the picture. Beyond the instrument are a ship\'s rail, a taut rope and the hard line of the sea\'s horizon with a low Sun on it, which the instrument is aimed at. Historically accurate: no numbers that can be read, no plastic, no later micrometer drum. The sextant is right of centre in the upper half. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain sky.' },
    birchbark: { cap: 'The northern forests of America', ll: [-89.68, 47.96], tx: 'Your Figures can walk two steps.', fact: 'The peoples of the northern forests of America built canoes of birch bark, stitched with spruce root and sealed with gum over a light wooden frame. A small one weighs about fifty pounds, no more than a child of seven, so a traveller could paddle to the end of one river and carry the canoe to the next.', art: 'A birchbark canoe of the Ojibwe, about 1600, being carried over a portage trail between two lakes, seen from the side: one man walks with the canoe upside down on his shoulders, its middle bar across them and his hands holding the edges, so that it covers him to the chest. The canoe is about fourteen feet long, more than twice his height, with both ends curving up to a blunt point; its bark is pale gold with dark horizontal flecks, sewn in panels with neat stitches of root, the seams sealed with black gum. He wears a hide shirt, leggings and moccasins and has a paddle tied inside the canoe. Behind him a woman of the same people follows with a pack on a strap across her forehead. Birch and spruce trees, a rocky path, bright water ahead. Only these two people. Historically accurate: no feather headdress, no painted designs on the bark, no metal fittings. The canoe runs across the upper half. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain sky and leaves.' },
    ropebridge: { cap: 'The Apurímac River, Peru', ll: [-71.48, -14.38], tx: 'Your Figures can walk two steps.', fact: 'The Inca crossed the gorges of the Andes on hanging bridges made of twisted grass. One of them still hangs over the Apurímac River in Peru, about thirty yards from cliff to cliff: every June the people of four villages cut the old bridge down and weave a new one in three days.', art: 'A hanging bridge of twisted grass rope across a deep, narrow river gorge in the high Andes, seen from one end looking along it: four cables as thick as a man\'s thigh form the floor, laid with a mat of sticks, and two thinner cables at waist height form the handrails, laced to the floor with a web of cord on each side, so that the whole bridge sags in a long curve from stone footings on this cliff to stone footings on the far one. The rope is the colour of straw, fresh and bright. One woman in a full dark skirt, a woven shawl and a flat hat is halfway across, small against the gorge, holding both rails; nobody else is on it. Far below, green river water; above, bare brown slopes. Historically accurate: no steel wire, no planks, no metal posts. The bridge runs from the lower left up to the right of centre. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain sky and rock.' },
    wheel: { cap: 'Ljubljana Marshes, about 3150 BC', ll: [14.4, 45.97], tx: 'Your Figures can walk two steps.', fact: 'The oldest wooden wheel yet found was dug out of a marsh in Slovenia in 2002 with its axle beside it. It is about 5,200 years old and is made of two planks of ash. Its square hole shows that wheel and axle were wedged together and turned as one.', art: 'The oldest wooden wheel known, as it was about 3150 BC, on a two-wheeled cart standing at the edge of a lake village in central Europe, seen close from the side and a little below: a solid disc of pale ash wood about two and a half feet across, made of two planks joined edge to edge by four dark wooden battens let into grooves, with a square hole at the centre in which a square-ended oak axle is wedged tight. The cart is a simple wooden frame with a pole in front; one ox, in true proportion, stands yoked to it with its head turned away. Mud and reeds underfoot, the stilts of a house on posts over the water behind. No people. Historically accurate: no spokes, no metal rim, no nails. The wheel fills the middle and right of the picture in the upper half. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain sky.' },
    quipu: { cap: 'The Andes', ll: [-71.97, -13.52], tx: 'Draw one more card each turn.', fact: 'The Inca kept the counts of a whole empire, its people, its llamas and its stores of maize, on knotted cords instead of in writing. The kind of knot and its place on the cord gave the ones, tens, hundreds and thousands. Herders in the Andes still keep count of their animals this way.', art: 'An Inca quipu of about 1450 held up and spread out by two hands, seen close from the front: one thick main cord held level between the hands, and from it some forty thin cords of cotton hanging straight down like a fringe, dyed in plain colours, cream, brown, dark blue, red and green, each with clusters of small knots tied at three or four different heights so that the knots line up in rows across the fringe. A few of the hanging cords have a shorter cord tied to them. Only the hands and the edge of a tunic woven with small squares are in the picture. Behind, out of focus, a wall of close-fitted grey stone and a row of storehouses on a hillside. Historically accurate: no beads, no feathers, no writing. The cords hang right of centre in the upper half. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain stone wall and sky.' },
    starchart: { cap: 'Dunhuang, China, about 670', ll: [94.8, 40.04], tx: 'Draw one more card each turn.', fact: 'This paper scroll from Tang dynasty China is the oldest complete map of the night sky known from anywhere. It shows more than 1,300 stars in ink, group by group, and it lay with some forty thousand other manuscripts in a sealed cave until 1907.', art: 'A Chinese star chart of about the year 670, a long narrow scroll of fine yellowed paper, partly unrolled on a low wooden table and seen from above at a slant: across it run groups of stars drawn as small circles in black, red and white-with-a-black-rim, joined into figures by thin straight ink lines, with a short column of brush writing beside each group, and at the unrolled end a round map of the stars about the pole. One hand in a wide grey sleeve holds the roll open while the other points with a thin bamboo stick at a group shaped like a ladle. An oil lamp, an ink stone and a brush stand at the edge; through a doorway behind are desert cliffs under a real night sky. No face. Historically accurate: no writing that can be read, no constellation pictures of animals or people, no telescope. The scroll fills the middle of the picture. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain dark wall and night sky.' },
    telescope: { cap: 'Padua, 1609', ll: [11.88, 45.41], tx: 'Draw one more card each turn.', fact: 'Galileo\'s telescope was a tube with a glass lens at each end that made things look twenty times closer. In January 1610 he watched four small lights beside Jupiter change places from night to night, and understood that they were moons circling another world.', art: 'Galileo\'s telescope of 1609 on a wooden stand at an open window in Padua, at night, seen close from the side: a slender tube about as long as a walking stick and no thicker than a wrist, made of wood covered in dark red leather with bands of gold tooling, a little wider where the lens sits at the far end, resting in a simple forked stand and pointed up at the sky. On the windowsill beside it lies an open notebook with a row of small ink drawings, each a circle with three or four dots beside it in different places. Outside, over tiled roofs and one dome, a single bright planet stands in a clear dark sky with four tiny points of light in a line beside it. No people. Historically accurate: no tripod of metal, no eyepiece at the side, no mirror. The tube runs from the lower left to the upper right of centre. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain night sky and window frame.' },
    manuscripts: { cap: 'Timbuktu, 13th to 19th centuries', ll: [-3, 16.77], tx: 'Draw one more card each turn.', fact: 'Families in Timbuktu have handed down handwritten books for hundreds of years, most of them in Arabic. When armed men seized the city in 2012, its librarians packed some 350,000 of them into ordinary metal trunks and sent them south to safety by road and by canoe on the Niger.', art: 'A manuscript of Timbuktu lying open in a tin trunk, seen close from above at a slant: a book of loose leaves of thick cream paper, not sewn, kept in a worn wrapper of red-brown tooled leather with a flap, its two open pages covered with neat lines of brown and black handwriting that runs from right to left, with notes written slantwise in the margins and one diagram of circles in red. The trunk is an ordinary painted metal one, half full of other books in their leather wrappers, stacked flat. A pair of hands in the sleeves of a pale blue robe is lowering one more book in. Mud-brick wall, a strip of hard sunlight across the floor, a little sand. No face. Historically accurate: no writing that can be read, no printed pages, no gold. The open book is right of centre in the upper half. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain mud wall.' },
    telegraph: { cap: 'Washington to Baltimore, 1844', ll: [-77.01, 38.89], tx: 'Draw one more card each turn.', fact: 'On 24 May 1844 Samuel Morse tapped out a message in dots and dashes at the Capitol in Washington, and it was read in Baltimore at the same moment. By 1861 a wire ran across the United States, and five years later another lay under the Atlantic.', art: 'A telegraph key and register of 1844 on a plain table, seen close from the side: a small brass lever on a wooden base, with a round black knob that one finger is pressing down, wired to a brass machine beside it in which a clockwork roller is drawing a narrow paper tape past a pointed arm; the tape curls out across the table marked with a row of short and long dents. Two cloth-covered wires run from the machine up the wall and out through a window frame to a wooden pole with glass knobs. Only the hand, a white cuff and a dark coat sleeve are in the picture. An oil lamp, a glass jar battery with metal plates. Historically accurate: no telephone, no light bulb, no letters or numbers that can be read. The key and the tape are right of centre in the upper half. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain wall.' },
    braille: { cap: 'Paris, 1824', ll: [2.35, 48.85], tx: 'Draw one more card each turn.', fact: 'Louis Braille lost his sight as a small boy. At school in Paris he was shown a soldier\'s code of raised dots for reading in the dark, and by the age of fifteen he had cut it down to a cell of six dots that one fingertip can cover. Those six dots make 63 different signs.', art: 'A page of braille being read by touch about 1830, seen very close from the side and a little above: a sheet of thick cream paper on a sloping wooden desk, covered with rows of small raised dots that catch the light from a window so that each dot throws a tiny shadow, and across one row the first two fingers of a boy\'s hand moving from left to right, the fingertips resting lightly on the dots. Beside the page lie the tools that made it: a flat brass frame with rows of small windows, hinged over another sheet, and a short awl with a round wooden handle. Only the hands and the cuffs of a dark school uniform are in the picture. Historically accurate: no printed letters, no pen and ink on the page, no spectacles. The page and the fingers are right of centre in the upper half. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain wall and window light.' },
    tablet: { cap: 'Uruk, about 3200 BC', ll: [45.64, 31.32], tx: 'Draw one more card each turn.', fact: 'The oldest writing we know is on small tablets of clay from the city of Uruk, in what is now Iraq. They are not stories or prayers but accounts: so much grain, so many sheep, so much beer for the workers. About six thousand of these earliest tablets have been found.', art: 'One of the earliest written tablets, from Uruk about 3200 BC, held in the palm of a hand and seen very close: a small cushion of pale brown clay no bigger than a playing card, its face divided into boxes by scratched lines, and in each box a few simple signs pressed in while the clay was wet, a jar with a pointed base, an ear of barley, a head beside a bowl, with rows of round and wedge-shaped dents beside them for numbers. The other hand holds a cut reed with its tip resting in the last box. Only the hands and bare forearms are in the picture. Behind, out of focus, big clay jars standing in rows against a mud-brick wall. Historically accurate: pictures and number dents, not the later wedge writing; nothing that reads as a modern letter or numeral. The tablet is right of centre in the upper half. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain mud-brick wall.' },
    voyagingcanoe: { cap: 'The Pacific Ocean', ll: [-149.4, -17.6], tx: 'Once a turn, jump one of your Figures to any Place. It is done for the turn.', fact: 'Polynesian voyagers crossed thousands of miles of open Pacific in canoes with two hulls, steering by the stars, the swells and the flight of birds. A canoe some sixty feet long could carry two dozen people with their pigs, dogs, chickens and young plants, enough to settle an island.', art: 'A Polynesian voyaging canoe far out on the open Pacific about a thousand years ago, just after sunset, seen from the water astern and to one side: two long slender hulls joined side by side by a platform of lashed poles, with a small thatched shelter on it, driven by two tall sails shaped like a crab\'s claw, woven of pale matting. The canoe rides up the face of a long ocean swell. In the stern the navigator stands braced at a great steering paddle, an older man in a wrapped bark-cloth garment, his face lifted to the first bright star low ahead. Three other people are aboard, each different: a woman tending young plants wrapped in leaves, a boy bailing, a man asleep against a basket; a pig and gourds of water beside them. Two white sea birds fly the same way overhead. Historically accurate: two hulls, not one hull with a float; no carved figurehead, no flower garlands, no compass. The sails are right of centre in the upper half. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain darkening sky.' },
    longship: { cap: 'Gokstad, Norway, about 890', ll: [10.25, 59.14], tx: 'Once a turn, jump one of your Figures to any Place. It is done for the turn.', fact: 'A longship was long, light and so shallow that it could be rowed up a river or run straight onto a beach. One built in Norway about 890, with places for 32 oars, was dug out of a burial mound almost whole in 1880, and in 1893 a copy of it crossed the Atlantic in about four weeks.', art: 'A Viking ship of about 890, the one found at Gokstad, under sail on a grey northern sea, seen from the water off her bow and a little below: a long, low, open ship of overlapping oak planks, about fourteen times as long as a man is tall, sharp and high at both ends with plain curved stems, a single mast with one great square sail of wool in broad stripes, and a row of round shields, alternately black and yellow, hung along the rail. A big steering oar is fixed to the right side near the stern, held by the one man standing there; four other men, each different, sit on sea chests or haul on a rope. Spray, gulls, a line of mountains far off. Historically accurate: no horned helmets, no dragon head on this ship, no cabin or deck house. The sail is right of centre in the upper half. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain cloudy sky.' },
    // Deeds
    teutoburg: { cap: 'Deed', tx: 'One of your Figures clashes with an enemy at its Place with +1 Might, even if it has already acted.', fact: 'In AD 9 three Roman legions were strung out along a narrow track between a wooded hill and a bog, in the rain, when Arminius\'s warriors struck from behind a wall of turf. Traces of that wall and thousands of lost Roman things have been dug up at Kalkriese in Germany.', art: 'The ambush in the Teutoburg Forest in September of AD 9, seen from low on the track: a narrow, muddy path between a steep wooded slope on the left and a reedy bog on the right, in heavy rain. Along the foot of the slope runs a low wall of cut turf topped with wattle, and over it three Germanic warriors, each different in age and build, with long narrow shields and spears, are coming down onto the path. Facing them are three Roman soldiers, each different, caught out of order: one in a mail shirt still pulling the leather rain cover off his big curved shield, one dragging at a baggage mule, one turning with his short sword half drawn. In the mud at the lower right lies a Roman cavalryman\'s face mask of silvered iron, fallen and staring up, rain beading on it. The rest of both armies is hidden by rain and trees. No horned or winged helmets, no close view of wounds, no rows or columns of repeated figures. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain dark wet branches.' },
    testudo: { cap: 'Quick Deed', tx: 'Quick. One of your Figures gets +2 Might for one clash. On the other player\'s turn it plays itself when it would save your Figure, paid for with 1 Spark you kept.', fact: 'Testudo is Latin for tortoise. Roman soldiers locked their shields edge to edge in front, at the sides and over their heads, and walked up to an enemy wall under a rain of arrows and stones.', art: 'A Roman tortoise of shields about 54 BC, seen from the wall it is attacking, looking down: a tight block of some thirty soldiers has become a single roof of long oval shields, overlapping like tiles, with a wall of shields along the front and sides and only sandalled feet and the tips of a few short swords showing at the edges. Stones and javelins thrown from above are bouncing off the shield roof, one in mid-air, and a clay pot has just smashed across it. The block moves steadily up a ramp of piled brushwood toward us. Nobody else is in the picture: the formation is the subject, small enough to be seen whole. Every shield carries the same painted pattern. Seen from above and in front, the formation in the middle and upper half of the picture. The late Roman Republic: oval shields curved like a section of barrel, mail shirts, plain bronze helmets; no rectangular shields, no segmented plate. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain earth bank and sky.' },
    brownsbox: { cap: 'Deed', tx: 'A Figure costing 3 or less arrives from your hand at any Place.', fact: 'In 1849 Henry Brown, enslaved in Virginia, had himself nailed into a wooden box about three feet long and sent by express to Philadelphia. He travelled twenty-seven hours by wagon, train and steamboat, part of the way upside down, and stepped out a free man.', art: 'Henry Brown\'s box on its way north in March 1849: a plain crate of rough boards, about three feet long and two wide, bound with five hoops of hickory, standing among trunks, mail sacks and barrels in the dim baggage car of a moving train. A paper label is pasted on its lid, and three small holes are bored in one end. Two railway porters in caps and aprons are sitting on it, one at each end, sharing a pipe, with no idea what is under them. A swinging lantern lights the car; through the half-open sliding door the night country streams past. In one of the three small holes, if one looks closely, a little lamplight catches the white of an eye. Seen from across the car, the crate in the middle of the picture and the porters\' heads in the upper half. Historically accurate: no bars, no chains, no lettering that can be read. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain dark roof of the car.' },
    beagle: { cap: 'Deed', tx: 'Draw 2 cards.', fact: 'HMS Beagle was a small survey ship, only about ninety feet long, sent to chart the coasts of South America. Her voyage was meant to take two years and lasted almost five.', art: 'HMS Beagle in 1834, lying at anchor in a narrow channel at the far southern tip of South America: a small, sturdy wooden ship with three masts, her sails furled, a white band along her dark hull and boats slung at her sides and stern. She is dwarfed by what stands behind her: a wall of dark beech forest rising straight from the water, and above it a great mountain of blue ice and snow whose glacier comes down to the sea. One of the ship\'s boats is pulling for the shore with a few men and a pile of boxes and nets. On the cramped deck and hanging in the rigging, small but plain to see, are the collector\'s things: crates, glass jars, a net, bones laid out to dry. Cold clear light, seabirds, a whale\'s back in the channel. Seen from the water, the ship in the middle and the mountain in the upper half. Historically accurate: no steam funnel, no gun smoke. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain cloud on the mountain.' },
    goldrush: { cap: 'Deed', tx: 'Gain 3 Spark.', fact: 'Gold was found at a sawmill in California in January 1848. Within two years about a hundred thousand people had come by wagon and by ship, from as far away as Chile, China and Australia.', art: 'A miner\'s pan in a California river in 1849, seen close from above: a wide, shallow iron pan held tilted in two wet, cracked hands just under the surface of clear fast water, with a crescent of black sand left along its lower edge, and in the black sand a scatter of bright yellow flakes and one small nugget. The water swirling out of the pan carries away the pale gravel. Around the hands are river stones, a pick, a worn boot planted in the stream. In the upper half of the picture the river runs away up a canyon past a wooden rocker box, two canvas tents, a mule and pine trees, and three other diggers, each different: a Chinese miner in a wide hat, a Chilean in a poncho, a sailor. Hard mountain light. No faces close up. Historically accurate: no machinery, no town. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain pines and sky.' },
    truce: { cap: 'Deed', tx: 'Enemy Figures at one Place miss their next turn.', fact: 'On Christmas Eve of 1914, in the first winter of the First World War, soldiers in the trenches of both sides began singing carols across the lines. In many places they climbed out next day to shake hands, swap small gifts and bury their dead, and here and there kicked a football about.', art: 'Christmas morning of 1914 between the trenches in Flanders: a flat field white with frost under a pale sun, crossed by two tangles of barbed wire. Out in the middle stand six soldiers, three of each army and each one different, their rifles left behind: men in khaki greatcoats and soft caps, and men in grey with round caps or a spiked helmet with a cloth cover. One of each is shaking hands; two more are trading a tin and a handful of cigars, and one has put on the other\'s helmet and is being laughed at. Along the parapet of the farther trench stands a row of tiny fir trees with candles. Nobody else is in the picture. Breath smokes in the cold. Seen from the lip of the nearer trench, the meeting in the middle of the picture. No fighting, no bodies, no tanks. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain pale winter sky.' },
    magnacarta: { cap: 'Deed', tx: 'Enemy Figures at one Place miss their next turn.', fact: 'In 1215 the barons of England forced King John to put his seal to a long list of promises, among them that no free man would be jailed except by the law of the land. Four copies written out that summer still exist.', art: 'The sealing of Magna Carta in a meadow beside the Thames in June 1215, seen close across a table: a single large sheet of parchment covered edge to edge with small, close writing lies on a cloth, and a clerk\'s hands are pressing a lump of soft green wax, hung from the foot of the sheet on a cord, between the two halves of a great seal press. Around the table stand the people who matter, cut off at the chest so that we see only hands and belts: on one side several men in mail with their gloved hands resting on their sword hilts, on the other a hand heavy with rings that grips the table\'s edge, and between them a bishop\'s hand raised to keep the peace. Grass, a striped tent pole, river light. No faces. Historically accurate: the king does not sign with a pen, no crown on the table, no writing that can be read. The parchment and the seal are in the middle of the picture. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain tent cloth.' },
    marathonmarch: { cap: 'Deed', tx: 'Make one of your Figures ready, so it can act again.', fact: 'After beating the Persians on the beach at Marathon in 490 BC, the Athenians saw the enemy fleet put to sea and make for their undefended city. They hurried the twenty-five miles home, and were waiting above the shore when the ships arrived.', art: 'The Athenian army after the battle of Marathon in 490 BC, hurrying home to save its city, seen from the road ahead: four tired Greek foot soldiers coming toward us along a dusty coast road between pines, each different in age and build, bronze helmets pushed back on their heads, big round shields slung on their backs, long spears on their shoulders, grey with dust and sweat and none of them stopping. The nearest, a man of thirty-five with a bandaged forearm, drinks from a skin as he walks; behind him a grey-bearded man has his arm around a limping boy of eighteen, and a fourth looks out to sea. On the bright water to the right, level with them and going the same way, three dark ships under oar and sail are racing them along the coast. The sun is low. Greece in 490 BC: no lone runner collapsing, no capes, no letters on the shields, no column of repeated figures behind them. The nearest man\'s head is right of centre, a fifth of the way down. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain sky and pine branches.' },
    ninth: { cap: 'Deed', tx: 'Make one of your Figures ready, so it can act again.', fact: 'At the first performance of his Ninth Symphony in 1824, Beethoven stood beside the conductor beating time, too deaf to hear the orchestra or the cheering. One of the singers turned him around by the sleeve so that he could see the audience on its feet.', art: 'The end of the first performance of the Ninth Symphony, in a theatre in Vienna on 7 May 1824, seen from the back of the stage: in the middle, with his back to the hall, stands a stocky grey-haired man in a dark green coat, still bent over a score on a stand and still beating time with one hand, though the music has stopped. A young woman, one of the solo singers, in a pale high-waisted gown, has stepped up beside him and taken his sleeve in her fingers, and is gently turning him by the arm to face the house. Past them, over the footlights, the hall is a blur of gold light and of white handkerchiefs waved in the air, so that he can see the applause he cannot hear; no faces can be made out. Two players of the orchestra, each different, have lowered their bows and are watching him. Seen from among the choir, the two figures from the knees up in the middle, the hall blazing in the upper half. No piano, no laurel wreath. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain dark stage curtain.' },
    saltmarch: { cap: 'Deed', tx: 'You count 4 more Might at a Place where you have a Figure, until the end of the round.', fact: 'The march began with Gandhi and 78 companions and took 24 days. By the time they reached the sea the line of walkers was about two miles long, and within the year some sixty thousand people had gone to jail for making or selling salt.', art: 'The shore at Dandi in western India early in the morning of 6 April 1930, seen very close and low: a thin brown hand and forearm reaching down to lift a small lump of grey mud crusted white with salt from the wet sand at the edge of the sea. The arm comes from a short white homespun wrap; a tall bamboo staff is planted in the sand beside it, and a pair of worn sandals. Behind, out of focus, stand three companions in white homespun and white caps, each different in age and build, one a woman in a white sari with her hands pressed together, and beyond them flat mud, small waves and a pale sunrise. No face in close view, no soldiers, no flags, no rows of repeated figures. The hand and the salt are right of centre in the upper half. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain dawn sky.' },
    emancipation: { cap: 'Deed', tx: 'You count 4 more Might at a Place where you have a Figure, until the end of the round.', fact: 'From 1 January 1863 the Proclamation declared free the people held as slaves in the states at war with the Union, and opened the army and navy to Black men. Nearly two hundred thousand of them served before the war ended.', art: 'Watch Night, 31 December 1862, in a small wooden church in the American South, a few minutes before midnight, seen from the middle of the room: an old Black man with white hair stands holding up a large silver pocket watch by its chain in the light of a single torch, and four people are gathered close around him, each different: a boy of ten with his hand raised, counting; a mother lifting a baby to see; a woman praying with her eyes shut; a young man in a soldier\'s blue coat at the open door. Everyone else in the church is lost in darkness. On the wall hangs a printed notice. All the light comes from the torch, gold on their faces. No chains, no overseer, no writing that can be read, no rows of repeated figures. The old man\'s head is right of centre, a fifth of the way down. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain dark rafters.' },
    planter: { cap: 'Deed', tx: 'Jump one of your Figures to any Place. It is done for the turn.', fact: 'Before dawn on 13 May 1862 Robert Smalls, the enslaved pilot of a Confederate steamer in Charleston, put on the captain\'s straw hat, picked up his own family and the families of his crew, and steamed out past the harbour forts, giving the right signal at each. He handed the ship to the Union fleet.', art: 'The steamer Planter leaving Charleston harbour at about half past four in the morning of 13 May 1862, seen from the water off her bow: a low wooden side-wheel steamer with one tall black funnel, a big paddle box on each side and a small wheelhouse on top, moving quietly through grey water in the half-dark before dawn. At the open window of the wheelhouse stands one man, a young Black man of twenty-three in a captain\'s white jacket and a wide straw hat pulled low, his arms folded on the sill the way the captain stood, one hand reaching for the whistle cord. No one else is on deck. Behind and above the ship rises the dark brick wall of a fort on its island, with one lantern and one small sentry on the parapet. Historically accurate: no flag flying yet, no smoke of gunfire, no people in chains. The wheelhouse and the man are right of centre in the upper half. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain dark sky.' },
    hieroglyphs: { cap: 'Deed', tx: 'Draw 2 cards.', fact: 'In the ancient writing of Egypt the names of kings and queens are ringed with an oval. Champollion matched the signs inside two of those ovals, Ptolemy and Cleopatra, with the same names in Greek, and so had the sounds of a dozen signs. With them he went on to read names that no Greek had written down.', art: 'A scholar\'s desk in Paris in September 1822, seen close from above at a slant: a large sheet of paper on which two long ovals have been copied in ink, each holding a short row of small Egyptian picture signs, a lion, a mouth, a reed, a square, a bird, and under each sign a pencilled Greek letter, with lines drawn from the signs the two ovals share. One hand holds a pencil on the last sign; the other pins down a printed drawing of a carved stone. Around the sheet lie a candle burned low, a stack of worn grammars, a cup, and a small plaster cast of a carved oval. Only the hands and frayed cuffs are in the picture. Grey morning light from a window. Historically accurate: the signs are true hieroglyphs in two ovals, no pyramids, no mummy, no writing in modern letters that can be read. The sheet is right of centre in the upper half. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain wall and window.' },
    halley: { cap: 'Deed', tx: 'Make one of your Figures ready, so it can act again.', fact: 'Edmond Halley worked out that one comet had been seen three times, about 76 years apart, and said it would be back around 1758. He died in 1742. On Christmas night of 1758 a farmer near Dresden who watched the sky as a hobby found it with his own telescope, the first return of a comet ever foretold.', art: 'Christmas night of 1758 on a farm near Dresden, seen from the yard: a broad-shouldered farmer of about thirty-five in a heavy coat, knitted cap and wooden clogs stands in the snow with his eye to a long brass telescope that rests on the top bar of a gate, pointed low over the roof of a timbered barn. In the clear black sky over the barn, among sharp winter stars, hangs a small soft patch of light with a short faint tail. A lantern stands in the snow at his feet beside an open notebook, and his breath smokes. Nobody else; one warm window in the farmhouse behind. Historically accurate: the comet is small and dim, not a blazing streak; no observatory dome, no wig. The comet is in the upper half, right of centre; the man stands lower left. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain black sky with a few stars.' },
    naovictoria: { cap: 'Deed', tx: 'Gain 3 Spark.', fact: 'On 6 September 1522 one small, leaking ship, the Victoria, reached Spain from the south with eighteen men on board, three years after sailing west. She was the first ship to go around the world. The cloves in her hold were worth more than the whole fleet of five ships had cost.', art: 'The ship Victoria coming into the mouth of a Spanish river in September 1522, seen from a small boat alongside: a short, round-bellied wooden ship with three masts, her paint gone, her sails patched and grey, weed along her waterline, and a thin stream of water running from a pump over her side. At the rail stand three of her crew, each different and all of them thin and bearded in rags, one shading his eyes toward the shore, one holding a pump handle, one with a candle. In the foreground, in the small boat, an open sack spills dark brown cloves, each like a small nail with a round head, across a plank. Low sandy shore, a white church tower, hard afternoon light. Historically accurate: no gun smoke, no crowd on the quay, no later galleon\'s high carved stern. The ship\'s hull and masts are right of centre in the upper half. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain sky.' },
    syracuse: { cap: 'Quick Deed', tx: 'Quick. One of your Figures gets +2 Might for one clash. On the other player\'s turn it plays itself when it would save your Figure, paid for with 1 Spark you kept.', fact: 'When a Roman fleet came against Syracuse in 213 BC, the historian Polybius says, beams swung out over the walls and dropped great weights on the ships, and iron claws caught others by the bow and stood them on end. The city held out for more than a year.', art: 'The sea wall of Syracuse in 213 BC, seen from the water below and to one side: a high wall of pale stone blocks rising straight from the sea, and over its top a great wooden beam, as long as the wall is tall, swung out on a pivot like the arm of a crane. From the beam\'s end a chain runs down to an iron grapple with three hooks that has caught the bow of one Roman warship and is hauling it up out of the water, so that the ship stands tilted on its stern with its oars hanging loose and water pouring off it. Three soldiers, each different, cling to its rail. On the wall, small, two men haul on the beam\'s other end with ropes. A second ship is backing away at the lower left. Historically accurate: one ship lifted, no mirrors, no fire, no rows of identical soldiers. The beam and the lifted bow are right of centre in the upper half. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain sky.' },
    penicillin: { cap: 'Deed', tx: 'Draw 2 cards.', fact: 'In September 1928 Alexander Fleming came back from holiday to his laboratory in London and found that mould had got into one of his dishes of bacteria. Around the mould the bacteria had gone. The medicine made from that mould, penicillin, was ready for patients in the 1940s.', art: 'A round glass dish held up to a window in a London hospital laboratory in September 1928, seen very close: a shallow dish about as wide as a hand, its floor covered with a thin layer of clear jelly dotted all over with small cream-coloured spots, and near one edge a single large fluffy patch of pale blue-green mould with a white rim. All around that patch there is a clear ring where the spots have vanished. Two fingers and a thumb hold the dish by its edge; a white coat cuff shows. Behind, out of focus, a crowded bench of other dishes in stacks, a brass microscope, a rack of glass tubes, a window with brick chimneys beyond. No face. Historically accurate: glass, not plastic; no rubber gloves, no printed labels that can be read. The dish is right of centre in the upper half. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain wall and window.' },
    folio: { cap: 'Deed', tx: 'Draw 2 cards.', fact: 'In 1623 John Heminges and Henry Condell, who had acted beside Shakespeare, had 36 of his plays printed in one large book. About half of them had never been printed before, among them Macbeth, Julius Caesar, Twelfth Night and The Tempest, and without the book they would probably be lost.', art: 'A printer\'s workshop in London in 1623, seen close across a table: a tall stack of large printed sheets, folded once and gathered but not yet bound, the top sheet showing a page of two columns of small print inside ruled borders, and beside the stack one finished copy bound in plain brown calfskin, as tall as a forearm, lying shut. Two men stand over them, seen from the chest down so that we see hands and aprons more than faces: one older, in an actor\'s good black doublet, laying a bundle of worn handwritten playbooks tied with string on the table; the other a printer in an inky apron, lifting a fresh sheet by its corners. Behind them the wooden frame and screw of a press and sheets hung over cords to dry. Historically accurate: no writing that can be read, no portrait showing, no quill being used. The stack and the book are right of centre in the upper half. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain dark beams.' },
    david: { cap: 'Deed', tx: 'You count 4 more Might at a Place where you have a Figure, until the end of the round.', fact: 'Michelangelo\'s David is seventeen feet tall, about three times the height of a man. It took forty men four days to move it the half mile from his workshop to the main square of Florence, hanging upright in a wooden cage that rolled on greased beams. It was unveiled there on 8 September 1504.', art: 'The moving of Michelangelo\'s David through a street of Florence in 1504, seen from the street ahead and a little below: the white marble statue of a standing naked youth, about three times the height of the men around it, hangs upright from thick ropes inside a tall open cage of heavy timbers, swaying a little clear of its floor, its weight on the right leg, the left hand raised to the shoulder, the head turned to its left. The cage stands on greased wooden beams laid on the paving. Four workmen, each different in age and dress, are at work: two lean on a lever at the back, one carries a beam forward, one pours grease from a pot. A boy watches from a doorway. Narrow street of plain stone houses, morning light on the marble. Historically accurate: the statue is finished and unbroken, no fig leaf, no pedestal yet, no rows of identical workers. The statue\'s head is right of centre, a fifth of the way down. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain sky between the roofs.' },
    rite: { cap: 'Deed', tx: 'Enemy Figures at one Place miss their next turn.', fact: 'At the first night of Stravinsky\'s ballet The Rite of Spring, in Paris on 29 May 1913, people in the audience shouted, whistled and argued so loudly that the dancers could not hear the orchestra. Most accounts say the man who had made the dance, Nijinsky, called the counts to them from the side of the stage.', art: 'The side of the stage at a Paris theatre on 29 May 1913, seen from the wings: in the near shadow a slight young man in evening dress stands on a wooden chair, one hand gripping a rope, the other beating the air, his mouth open, calling numbers toward the stage. Out in the light three dancers, each different, are caught in the middle of a heavy, flat-footed jump with their knees bent and feet turned in, wearing long belted smocks painted with bands of red and blue patterns, soft boots bound with cross-straps, and long braids or pointed caps. Beyond them, across the footlights, the dark hall shows only as a few white shirt fronts and raised programmes. Historically accurate: no tutus, no pointe shoes, no rows of identical dancers. The man\'s head is right of centre, a fifth of the way down. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain dark curtain.' },
    lincolnconcert: { cap: 'Deed', tx: 'Make one of your Figures ready, so it can act again.', fact: 'In 1939 the great singer Marian Anderson was refused the biggest concert hall in Washington because she was Black. So on Easter Sunday she sang from the steps of the Lincoln Memorial instead, in the open air, to 75,000 people and to millions more by radio.', art: 'Marian Anderson singing on the steps of the Lincoln Memorial on 9 April 1939, seen from the side and a little below: a tall, composed Black woman of forty-two in a long dark fur coat, dressed as the photographs of that afternoon show her, standing very straight with her eyes closed and her hands clasped in front of her, before a cluster of six old-fashioned radio microphones on thin stands. Behind her rise two of the great fluted white columns of the memorial, and between them, in shadow, the seated marble figure can just be made out. A grand piano and its player, a man in a dark overcoat, are at the left edge. The listeners are not shown except as a soft band of colour far below. Cold bright afternoon. She died within living memory, so this is a respectful, recognisable portrait taken from the photographs of that afternoon, with nothing invented. Historically accurate: no stage, no banner, no modern loudspeakers, no rows of faces. Her head is right of centre, a fifth of the way down. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain pale sky and stone.' },
    pictureshow: { cap: 'Deed', tx: 'Gain 3 Spark.', fact: 'On 28 December 1895, in a basement room of a café in Paris, the brothers Auguste and Louis Lumière showed ten films, each less than a minute long, to people who had paid one franc. Fewer than forty came that first evening.', art: 'The first paid film show, in a basement room in Paris on 28 December 1895, seen from behind the projector: in the foreground a wooden box no bigger than a hat box stands on a tall stand, with a brass lens in front and a small crank handle that one hand is turning, a lamp house behind it throwing a cone of light through the dark room. On a white cloth screen at the far end is a grey moving picture of a factory gate with people walking out of it, among them a woman in a wide hat and a man pushing a bicycle. Between the projector and the screen sit only five spectators on cane chairs, seen from behind, each different: a man in a top hat leaning forward, a woman with her hand to her mouth, a boy standing up. Historically accurate: the picture on the screen is black and white and silent, no train rushing at the audience, no rows of identical heads. The screen is right of centre in the upper half. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain dark wall.' },
    spies: { cap: 'Deed', tx: 'Swap two Figures at different Places. At least one must be yours.', fact: 'On the morning of 10 February 1962 two prisoners walked past each other on a bridge at the edge of Berlin, between East and West: Francis Gary Powers, an American pilot shot down over the Soviet Union, and Rudolf Abel, a Soviet spy caught in New York. Each went home.', art: 'The Glienicke Bridge on the cold morning of 10 February 1962, seen from one bank looking along it: a long steel bridge of green-painted girders in low arches, its roadway empty and wet, with a white line painted across the middle. On the line two men are passing each other, walking in opposite directions, each alone: one tall and young in a heavy coat and a Russian fur hat, carrying a small case; the other thin, older and bald, in a dark overcoat, hatless, carrying a bundle. They do not look at each other. At each end of the bridge, small and far apart, stand two men in overcoats and one parked car. Bare trees, mist on the river. Historically accurate: morning, not night; no searchlights, no soldiers in rows, no barbed wire on the bridge. The two men are right of centre in the upper half. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain grey sky.' },
    rubicon: { cap: 'Deed', tx: 'Jump one of your Figures to any Place. It is done for the turn.', fact: 'A Roman general had to give up his army before he came home to Italy. In January of 49 BC Julius Caesar crossed the little river Rubicon, the border, with a single legion at his back. From that step there was no going back, and it began a civil war.', art: 'The river Rubicon before dawn on a January morning in 49 BC, seen from the Italian bank, low at the water\'s edge: a stream so narrow and shallow that a man can wade it in ten strides, running between low banks of frosted reeds. Three soldiers of the late Republic are wading across toward us, each different in age and build, in mail shirts and plain bronze helmets, with oval shields slung on their backs: the first, a grey veteran, is already stepping out onto this bank with water streaming from his boots, the second carries a standard topped with a silver eagle, and the third, a boy, looks back over his shoulder at the bank they have left. On this side, in the mud at the lower left, are the fresh hoof prints of one horse that has gone on ahead, already filling with water. One torch burns on the far bank. Mist, a leafless willow, a few last stars. Historically accurate: the general himself is not in the picture, no laurel wreaths, no rectangular shields, no red crests in rows, no rows of identical soldiers. The eagle is right of centre, a fifth of the way down. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain night sky.' },
    adwa: { cap: 'Deed', tx: 'You count 4 more Might at a Place where you have a Figure, until the end of the round.', fact: 'Lords from every part of Ethiopia brought their soldiers to Adwa to stand with the emperor Menelik and the empress Taytu. On the night before 1 March 1896 three Italian columns set out in the dark along separate mountain tracks to surprise them. The Ethiopians were waiting, and by the end of the day the invaders were beaten.', art: 'The hills east of Adwa at first light on 1 March 1896, seen from an Ethiopian position looking down a rocky slope: in the foreground three Ethiopian soldiers, each different, wait behind boulders, a grey-bearded nobleman in a lion\'s-mane headdress and an embroidered cape with a curved sword and a round hide shield studded with silver, a young rifleman in a white cotton wrap with a cartridge belt aiming a breech-loading rifle, and a woman bringing a water gourd. On the next ridge a red, gold and green royal umbrella marks where the emperor and empress stand, small and far off. Down in the valley, small, a short broken line of soldiers in pale uniforms and tropical helmets is coming out of the mist. Steep flat-topped mountains behind. Historically accurate: rifles as well as spears and shields, no close view of wounds, no rows of identical soldiers. The nobleman\'s head is right of centre, a fifth of the way down. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain dawn sky.' },
    everest: { cap: 'Deed', tx: 'Jump one of your Figures to any Place. It is done for the turn.', fact: 'At half past eleven on the morning of 29 May 1953 the Sherpa climber Tenzing Norgay and the New Zealander Edmund Hillary became the first people known to stand on the highest point on Earth. They stayed about fifteen minutes, because their oxygen was running low.', art: 'The top of Mount Everest at half past eleven on 29 May 1953, seen from a few steps below the summit: a small rounded dome of snow against a sky so dark blue it is almost black, and on it one man standing, Tenzing Norgay, in a bulky blue windproof suit, high boots with metal spikes, thick gloves, a hood and an oxygen mask with goggles pushed up, holding his ice axe above his head with a string of four small flags blowing out from its shaft. A rope runs from his waist down to the lower left corner, where the gloved hand and the box camera of his partner come into the picture. Below and behind them the white ridges of other great mountains fall away under a floor of cloud. Only these two people, and only one of them seen whole. He died within living memory, and this is the photograph his partner took of him that morning, drawn respectfully with nothing invented. Historically accurate: 1953 clothing and a heavy oxygen frame on his back, no fixed ropes, no ladders, no other climbers. His head is right of centre, a fifth of the way down. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain dark blue sky.' },
    kadesh: { cap: 'Deed', tx: 'Enemy Figures at one Place miss their next turn.', fact: 'About 1259 BC the kings of Egypt and of the Hittites swore to end their long war and never to attack each other again. Their treaty is the oldest peace treaty whose words survive, and we have both sides\' copies: one carved on temple walls in Egypt, the other on clay tablets dug up in Turkey.', art: 'The treaty between Egypt and the Hittites as it was sent about 1259 BC, seen close on a table of dark wood: a flat tablet of bright silver about the size of a large book, covered with fine rows of small wedge-shaped signs, with a round seal pressed in the middle showing a tiny figure embraced by a taller one. Two hands hold it from opposite sides of the table: one a brown Egyptian hand with a gold bracelet and a pleated white linen sleeve, the other a paler hand in the long woollen sleeve of a Hittite envoy, wearing a seal ring. Between them on the table lie a clay tablet with the same wedge signs and a roll of papyrus. No faces. Sunlight through a doorway, a painted column behind. Historically accurate: wedge signs on the silver, no hieroglyphs on it, no weapons on the table, no writing that can be read. The tablet is right of centre in the upper half. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain wall.' },
    typhoon: { cap: 'Deed', tx: 'Enemy Figures at one Place miss their next turn.', fact: 'In the summer of 1281 a great fleet sent by Kublai Khan lay off the coast of Japan for weeks, kept from landing by a stone wall twelve miles long around Hakata Bay and by raids in small boats at night. In the middle of August a typhoon struck, and most of the ships were wrecked at their moorings.', art: 'The typhoon of August 1281 off the coast of Kyushu, seen from the top of the stone sea wall: in the foreground the wall itself, about as high as a man and a half, built of rough fitted stones, and on it two samurai, each different, crouched against the wind, one older in dark laced armour holding his helmet on, one young with a long bow, both looking out to sea. Out in the bay, under a black sky and flying spray, three big wooden ships with high sterns and ribbed matting sails have been driven together; one is rolling over, its mast gone, and broken planks and a great wooden anchor wash in the surf below the wall. Rain in sheets, one break of livid light on the horizon. Historically accurate: only three ships and two men, no lightning dragon, no rows of identical ships or soldiers. The ships are right of centre in the upper half. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain storm cloud.' },
    tomb: { cap: 'Deed', tx: 'Gain 3 Spark.', fact: 'On 4 November 1922 Howard Carter\'s workmen uncovered a stone step in the floor of the Valley of the Kings; by one account a boy carrying water found it. The steps led down to a sealed door. The last of what lay behind it was not carried out until 1932.', art: 'The first look into Tutankhamun\'s tomb on 26 November 1922, seen from inside the dark room looking back at the door: a wall of rough plaster blocks with a small ragged hole broken through its upper corner, and through the hole a hand holding a lit candle and, behind it, one eye and the brim of a hat. The candle\'s light falls into the room on three things only: the gilded head of a couch carved as a cow with tall horns, the side of a painted wooden chest, and a pile of white egg-shaped boxes. Everything else is black. Dust hangs in the beam. Historically accurate: no electric torch, no golden mask in view, no mummy, no writing that can be read. The hole and the candle are right of centre in the upper half. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain darkness.' },
    lascaux: { cap: 'Deed', tx: 'Draw 2 cards.', fact: 'In September 1940 Marcel Ravidat, who was eighteen, and three friends widened a hole left by a fallen tree near the village of Montignac in France and climbed down with a lamp. On the walls were horses, bulls and deer larger than life, painted by people who lived some 17,000 to 20,000 years before.', art: 'The painted cave of Lascaux on 12 September 1940, seen from the floor looking up: a boy of eighteen in a patched jacket, shorts and heavy boots stands at the lower left with his back half to us, holding up a home-made oil lamp, and its yellow light spreads over a curving wall and ceiling of pale rock covered with animals painted in black, red and ochre: one great bull drawn in black outline, three times as long as the boy is tall, facing a line of small galloping horses with bristly manes, and a red cow leaping above them. A second boy, younger and smaller, is only a pair of legs sliding in through a hole low at the left edge. Nobody else. Historically accurate: the animals are in the true style of the cave, no painted people, no handprints, no electric torch. The bull\'s head is right of centre in the upper half. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain dark rock.' },
  };
  Object.keys(P3).forEach(function (k) { T[k] = P3[k]; });
  const LINKS3 = {
    "@library>euclid": "Ancient writers place Euclid in Alexandria under the first Ptolemy, the king who began its Library.",
    "@library>eratosthenes": "Eratosthenes was the chief librarian of the Library of Alexandria.",
    "archimedes>eratosthenes": "Archimedes addressed his book The Method to his friend Eratosthenes.",
    "brahmagupta>alkhwarizmi": "Al-Khwarizmi's star tables follow the Sindhind, an Arabic version of Indian astronomy that is thought to come from Brahmagupta's book, and his own book on Indian numerals taught readers of Arabic to reckon with zero.",
    "alkhwarizmi>fibonacci": "Fibonacci learned the Indian numerals as a boy in North Africa, and his book draws on al-Khwarizmi and the Arab mathematicians who followed him.",
    "press>gutenberg": "Gutenberg built his press in Mainz about 1450 and printed his great Bible on it.",
    "paper>gutenberg": "Most copies of Gutenberg's Bible were printed on paper, which cost far less than calfskin.",
    "copernicus>kepler": "Kepler's first book, printed in 1596, was a defence of Copernicus's Sun-centred system.",
    "newton>duchatelet": "Émilie du Châtelet put Newton's Principia into French, and hers is still the standard French translation.",
    "montagu>jenner": "As a boy, Jenner was himself protected against smallpox by the method Lady Mary Wortley Montagu had brought to England.",
    "rosetta>champollion": "The Rosetta Stone, with its Greek text beside the Egyptian, gave Champollion the way in to reading hieroglyphs.",
    "jenner>pasteur": "Pasteur gave his own method the name vaccination, in honour of Jenner's work with cowpox.",
    "verne>bly": "Nellie Bly set out in 1889 to beat the eighty days of Jules Verne's novel, and stopped in France on the way to meet him.",
    "faraday>tesla": "Tesla's motors run on electromagnetic induction, the effect that Faraday discovered.",
    "newton>einstein": "Einstein kept a picture of Newton on the wall of his study, and his own theory of gravity took the place of Newton's.",
    "faraday>einstein": "Einstein's first paper on relativity begins with Faraday's magnet and coil of wire.",
    "ada>turing": "In 1950 Turing answered what he called Lady Lovelace's Objection, her remark that a machine can do only what we know how to order it to do.",
    "@wall>qinshihuang": "Qin Shi Huang had the old border walls of the northern kingdoms joined into one long wall, the first Great Wall.",
    "caesar>augustus": "Julius Caesar adopted Augustus, his great-nephew, in his will and left him his name.",
    "cyrus>darius": "Darius married Atossa, a daughter of Cyrus, and had it carved on the cliff at Behistun that he came of the same royal house.",
    "@machu>pachacuti": "Machu Picchu is thought to have been built as a royal estate for Pachacuti, about 1450.",
    "duchatelet>voltaire": "Voltaire lived and worked for fifteen years at the country house of Émilie du Châtelet, whose mathematics helped him to write his book on Newton.",
    "voltaire>catherine": "Catherine exchanged letters with Voltaire for fifteen years, and bought his library after he died.",
    "@seneca>stanton": "Elizabeth Cady Stanton lived in Seneca Falls, helped call the convention of 1848 and wrote most of its declaration.",
    "stanton>anthony": "Susan B. Anthony met Elizabeth Cady Stanton in 1851, and the two worked side by side for women's rights for fifty years.",
    "thoreau>king": "As a student at Morehouse College in Atlanta, Martin Luther King read Thoreau's essay on civil disobedience and went back to it again and again.",
    "caesar>vercingetorix": "Vercingetorix led the tribes of Gaul against Julius Caesar, and gave himself up to him at Alesia in 52 BC.",
    "augustus>amanirenas": "A bronze head of Augustus, cut from a statue that the army of Kush carried off, was found buried under the steps of a temple in Amanirenas's capital.",
    "augustus>arminius": "Arminius destroyed three of Augustus's legions, and the Roman writer Suetonius says the old emperor cried out for his general to give them back.",
    "eleanor>richard": "Richard was Eleanor's son. While he was held prisoner in 1193 she gathered the ransom that freed him.",
    "saladin>richard": "Richard and Saladin fought over the Holy Land for more than a year and made peace in 1192 without ever meeting face to face.",
    "magellan>lapulapu": "Magellan was killed in 1521 on the island of Mactan, fighting the warriors of its chief Lapulapu.",
    "hannibal>napoleon": "When Napoleon took his army over the Alps in 1800, his painter set Hannibal's name on the rocks beside his own.",
    "caesar>napoleon": "In his last years on Saint Helena, Napoleon dictated a book on the wars of Julius Caesar.",
    "sargon>enheduanna": "Enheduanna was the daughter of Sargon of Akkad, who made her high priestess of the moon god at Ur.",
    "homer>aeschylus": "An ancient writer reports that Aeschylus called his own plays slices from the great banquet of Homer.",
    "murasaki>seishonagon": "Murasaki Shikibu and Sei Shōnagon served rival empresses, and Murasaki wrote in her diary that Sei was dreadfully conceited.",
    "galileo>gentileschi": "Artemisia Gentileschi came to know Galileo in Florence, and the two wrote to each other for years afterwards.",
    "libai>basho": "Bashō opens his travel diary by echoing Li Bai's line that the passing days and months are travellers too.",
    "homer>wheatley": "The first poem in Phillis Wheatley's book praises Homer by name.",
    "virgil>wheatley": "Phillis Wheatley learned Latin in her teens, and in the first poem of her book she wishes she could sing as Virgil did.",
    "hokusai>hiroshige": "Hiroshige turned to landscape after Hokusai's views of Mount Fuji had made such prints the fashion in Edo.",
    "shakespeare>goethe": "Goethe wrote that the first page of Shakespeare he read made him Shakespeare's for life.",
    "mozart>tchaikovsky": "Tchaikovsky loved Mozart above all other composers, and built his suite Mozartiana from Mozart's tunes.",
    "hiroshige>vangogh": "Van Gogh collected Japanese prints, and in 1887 he copied two of Hiroshige's in oil paint to learn from them.",
    "elizabeth>grace": "Grace O'Malley sailed to England in 1593 and put her case to Queen Elizabeth face to face.",
    "wollstonecraft>shelley": "Mary Shelley was Mary Wollstonecraft's daughter. Her mother died days after she was born, and she grew up reading her books.",
    "poe>verne": "Jules Verne admired Edgar Allan Poe's tales, and wrote a sequel to Poe's story of a voyage toward the South Pole.",
    "sittingbull>oakley": "Sitting Bull saw Annie Oakley shoot in 1884, adopted her as a daughter, and gave her a name that means Little Sure Shot.",
    "hypatia>astrolabe": "Hypatia's father Theon wrote a book on the astrolabe that later writers built on, and one of her students thanked her for teaching him how to make one.",
    "cailun>paper": "Cai Lun showed the Han court in 105 how to make good paper from bark, hemp, rags and old nets.",
    "napoleon>rosetta": "Soldiers of the army Napoleon took to Egypt found the Rosetta Stone in 1799, built into an old wall they were pulling down.",
    "arminius>teutoburg": "Arminius led three Roman legions into the Teutoburg Forest in AD 9 and destroyed them there.",
    "caesar>testudo": "In his own account of invading Britain, Caesar tells how the Seventh Legion locked shields into a tortoise to storm a stronghold in the woods.",
    "douglass>brownsbox": "Frederick Douglass wished Henry Brown's escape in a box had been kept quiet, so that others could have got away by the same road.",
    "darwin>beagle": "Charles Darwin sailed as naturalist on HMS Beagle, and filled the ship with the rocks, bones and animals he collected.",
    "eleanor>magnacarta": "King John, who was made to put his seal to Magna Carta in 1215, was Eleanor of Aquitaine's youngest son.",
    "aeschylus>marathonmarch": "Aeschylus fought in the Athenian line at Marathon, where his brother was killed.",
    "beethoven>ninth": "The Ode to Joy is the last movement of Beethoven's Ninth Symphony, first played in Vienna in 1824.",
    "thoreau>saltmarch": "Gandhi printed parts of Thoreau's essay on civil disobedience in his own newspaper in 1907, twenty-three years before he walked to the sea.",
    "douglass>emancipation": "Frederick Douglass went to the White House in 1863 and again in 1864 to speak to Lincoln for the Black soldiers the Proclamation had brought into the army.",
    "astrolabe>sextant": "Before the sextant, sailors found how far north or south they were by measuring the height of the Sun with a seagoing astrolabe.",
    "pachacuti>ropebridge": "The Inca roads, with their hanging bridges, grew with the empire that Pachacuti began.",
    "@silk>starchart": "The chart was found in 1907 in a sealed cave at Dunhuang, an oasis town on the Silk Road.",
    "galileo>telescope": "Galileo heard in 1609 of a Dutch spyglass, built better ones himself, and turned them on the sky.",
    "@timbuktu>manuscripts": "The books were written and copied in Timbuktu, and families there have kept them from parent to child ever since.",
    "champollion>hieroglyphs": "On 27 September 1822 Champollion's letter was read out in Paris, showing how the hieroglyphs spell names by their sounds.",
    "rosetta>hieroglyphs": "The Greek on the Rosetta Stone told scholars what the Egyptian beside it had to say.",
    "newton>halley": "Edmond Halley used Newton's law of gravity to show that the comets of 1531, 1607 and 1682 were one comet coming back.",
    "magellan>naovictoria": "The Victoria was the last of the five ships that Magellan led out of Spain in 1519.",
    "archimedes>syracuse": "Archimedes designed the machines on the walls of Syracuse, his own city, when the Romans attacked it from the sea.",
    "shakespeare>folio": "Seven years after Shakespeare died, two actors of his own company gathered his plays into one book.",
    "press>folio": "About 750 copies were printed in London, and some 230 of them still exist.",
    "michelangelo>david": "Michelangelo was twenty-nine when his David was unveiled in Florence.",
    "caesar>rubicon": "In January of 49 BC Caesar led the Thirteenth Legion across the Rubicon, the small river that marked the edge of Italy.",
    "menelik>adwa": "Menelik II led the armies of Ethiopia at Adwa on 1 March 1896.",
    "tenzing>everest": "Tenzing Norgay reached the summit with Edmund Hillary on 29 May 1953, on his seventh expedition to the mountain.",
    "ramesses>kadesh": "Ramesses II made this peace with the Hittite king Hattusili III in the twenty-first year of his reign.",
    "kublai>typhoon": "Kublai Khan sent the fleet against Japan in 1281, seven years after his first one had turned back.",
    "tutankhamun>tomb": "The tomb was Tutankhamun's own, sealed some 3,250 years before and still full.",
    // (lines from the new cards to cards the game already had)
    "humboldt>darwin": "As a student Darwin read Humboldt's account of his travels over and over, and he took it with him on the Beagle.",
    "humboldt>bolivar": "The young Bolívar met Humboldt in Paris in 1804, soon after the explorer came back from South America.",
    "kublai>marcopolo": "Marco Polo says in his book that he served Kublai Khan for seventeen years.",
    "kepler>newton": "Newton showed that Kepler's three laws of the planets all follow from gravity.",
    "bach>mozart": "Mozart studied Bach's fugues in Vienna in 1782 and arranged some of them for strings.",
    "pythagoras>plato": "Plato visited the followers of Pythagoras in southern Italy, and their belief in number runs through his later books.",
  };
