  // ── The Domain cards (cards.js P4): eleven cards that act on the Domains themselves. Each fact is from
  // scripts/anoria/places/domain-cards.json (sure: 3 well attested, 2 the usual account, 1 a story, and the sentence
  // says so). The briefs follow the same notes as the third printing's.
  var P4 = {
    idrisi: { cap: 'Ceuta and Palermo, c. 1100 to 1165', ll: [13.36, 38.12], tx: 'On arrival, you may look through your whole Domain stack, choose one Domain and lay it at your Gate or your Outpost in place of the Domain that is there. Then shuffle your stack.', fact: 'At the court of Roger II in Palermo he spent some fifteen years questioning travellers, and in 1154 he finished a map of the known world with a book describing every part of it.', sure: 3,
      art: 'Al-Idrisi about 1150, in his fifties, at the court of the Norman king of Sicily in Palermo. Nobody knows his face, so he is a thoughtful, weathered man with a grey-flecked beard, in the long robe and wound turban of a scholar from Muslim Spain and Morocco. He kneels on a carpet over a large round map drawn on parchment, with south at the top as his own maps have it, the sea in blue and the mountains as small coloured humps, and marks a coast with a reed pen while he listens. Two travellers answer his questions, each different: a sunburnt ship\'s pilot counting off harbours on his fingers, and a merchant in a fur-edged coat unrolling a strip of notes. Behind them a wall glitters with gold mosaic of the kind the king\'s chapel has. His head is right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. No globe, no compass rose, no north at the top. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only gold mosaic ground there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    diogenes: { cap: 'Sinope and Corinth, c. 404 to 323 BC', ll: [22.93, 37.94], tx: 'The Domain where Diogenes stands has no rule.', fact: 'He lived in Athens and Corinth with next to nothing and mocked wealth, rank and custom. The story goes that when Alexander offered him any favour, he asked the king to stand out of his sunlight.', sure: 1,
      art: 'Diogenes of Sinope in old age, about 330 BC, in the marketplace of Corinth: a lean, sunburnt, bald old man with a ragged white beard and bright, mocking eyes, barefoot, wearing only a coarse cloak folded double, with a stick and a small leather bag beside him. He sits on the ground in the morning sun, leaning back against the huge clay storage jar he sleeps in, which lies on its side and is as tall as his shoulder as he sits, and with one hand he waves someone out of his light: the long shadow of a tall standing man, who is not in the picture, falls across the ground in front of him and stops at his feet. A thin dog lies beside him. His head is right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. Stone columns and market stalls behind, out of focus. A clay jar, not a wooden barrel. No lantern, no crown, no armour. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only clear sky there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    constantine: { cap: 'Naissus and Constantinople, c. 272 to 337', ll: [28.98, 41.01], tx: 'On arrival, you may turn up the top Domain of your stack and lay it at the Crossroads in place of the Domain that is there.', fact: 'In 330 he made the old Greek town of Byzantium, where Europe meets Asia, his new capital, and it was called Constantinople after him.', sure: 3,
      art: 'The emperor Constantine in the year 330, aged about fifty-eight, with the face of his colossal marble portrait in Rome: clean-shaven, a heavy square jaw, a strong hooked nose, large eyes looking up and far off, and hair combed forward in a fringe. He wears a band of pearls round his head, a long purple cloak pinned at the right shoulder with a jewelled brooch, and a gold-embroidered tunic. He stands on a terrace above the narrow blue strait at Byzantium with one hand held out flat, as if setting something down on the land below, where a new city is going up: a round forum with a tall column of dark red stone drums still in its scaffolding, and a line of new wall. Two surveyors work beside him, each different: an old one sighting along a cross-shaped surveying staff with hanging weights, a young one unrolling a plan. His head is right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. No halo, no cross in the sky, no domed church: none was built yet. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only sky there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    william: { cap: 'Normandy and England, c. 1028 to 1087', ll: [0.49, 50.91], tx: 'On arrival, if William is at the other player\'s Gate or Outpost, you may have the Domain there discarded. The other player lays the top Domain of their stack in its place.', fact: 'After he won at Hastings in 1066 he gave the land of England to his own followers, and his great survey of 1086, the Domesday Book, shows almost none of it still in English hands.', sure: 3,
      art: 'William, duke of Normandy, at the battle of Hastings on 14 October 1066, at the moment the Bayeux Tapestry shows: his men think he is dead, and he pushes his helmet back to show them his face. A strongly built man of about thirty-eight with a heavy, clean-shaven face and his dark hair shaved high at the back of the head in the Norman way, in a knee-length shirt of mail and a cone-shaped iron helmet with a nose bar, which his left hand tips up and back. In his right hand he holds a wooden club, as the tapestry gives him, not a sword. He sits a stocky stallion, turning in the saddle toward us, and the horse is in true proportion: its back level with a standing man\'s chest, its head as long as his arm. Two riders beside him, each different: a bishop in mail with a club of his own, and a young knight with a long shield shaped like a kite, pointing at him and shouting. His head is right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. No plate armour, no crown, no castle. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only grey sky there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    wren: { cap: 'London, 1632 to 1723', ll: [-0.098, 51.514], tx: 'On arrival, you may take a Domain from your Domain discard pile and lay it at your Gate or your Outpost in place of the Domain that is there.', fact: 'After the Great Fire of London in 1666 he rebuilt St Paul\'s Cathedral and more than fifty of the city\'s churches.', sure: 3,
      art: 'Sir Christopher Wren about 1700, in his late sixties, on the scaffolding of St Paul\'s Cathedral as its dome goes up: a small, slight, alert man with a long narrow face and a full curled wig to his shoulders, in a plain dark coat with a white neck cloth, as his portrait by Kneller shows him. He stands on a plank platform with a pair of brass dividers in one hand and a large drawing held open against the wind in the other, looking up at the work, pleased. Behind him rises the ring of pale stone columns that will carry the dome, about eight times a man\'s height, wrapped in timber scaffolding and ropes. Two masons work beside him, each different: a broad one in a leather apron setting a block with a mallet, a boy steadying a pulley rope. Far below, the new brick city and the spires of his churches, and the river. His head is right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. No finished dome, no flames. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only sky there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    earthquake: { cap: 'Rhodes, 226 BC', ll: [28.23, 36.45], tx: 'Choose a Gate or an Outpost, yours or the other player\'s. The Domain there is discarded, and the player whose side it is on lays the top Domain of their stack in its place.', fact: 'An earthquake in 226 BC threw down the Colossus of Rhodes, which had stood for little more than fifty years.', sure: 3,
      art: 'The earthquake at Rhodes in 226 BC: the Colossus, a bronze statue of the sun god about nineteen times a man\'s height, standing with its feet together on a block of white marble beside the harbour, not astride it, snaps at the knees, as an ancient writer says it did, and begins to topple, its rayed crown tilting against the sky and its iron frame showing in the break. Nobody knows how it stood, so the pose is simple: one arm raised to shade the eyes. The paving splits, the harbour water heaves and two moored ships crash together. In front, three people run, each different: a fisherman dropping his net, a woman dragging a child by the hand, an old man looking back over his shoulder. The statue\'s head is right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. No statue straddling the harbour mouth, no torch, no modern lighthouse. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only dusty sky there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    vesuvius: { cap: 'Bay of Naples, AD 79', ll: [14.43, 40.82], tx: 'Lay Vesuvius on a Domain. That Domain has no rule for as long as Vesuvius lies on it. Vesuvius goes wherever the Domain goes, and is discarded with it.', fact: 'In AD 79 it buried the trading town of Pompeii in a day, and the town lay under the ash for nearly seventeen hundred years.', sure: 3,
      art: 'Vesuvius on the afternoon the eruption began in AD 79, seen from a street in Pompeii looking toward the mountain: a single green peak, and standing on it a cloud shaped as the one eyewitness described it, like an umbrella pine, a tall straight trunk of grey smoke that spreads into flat branches at the top, dark in some places and pale in others. Small light stones are starting to fall and bounce on the street, which is paved with dark blocks and crossed by a row of raised stepping stones. Three people hurry away toward us, each different, each with a cushion tied on the head with a strip of cloth, as the eyewitness says people did: a baker with a child on his hip, a woman carrying a small bronze lamp, and an old man with a stick. Shop counters with round holes for jars line the street. The top of the cloud is right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. No rivers of red lava, no two-peaked mountain. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only pale sky there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    caperoute: { cap: 'Lisbon to Calicut, 1498', ll: [75.78, 11.25], tx: 'Turn up the top Domain of your stack and lay it at the Crossroads in place of the Domain that is there.', fact: 'Vasco da Gama reached India by sea around Africa in 1498, and within a few years a large share of the spice trade that had made Venice and Cairo rich went by the new route.', sure: 2,
      art: 'The end of the first voyage from Europe to India round Africa, off Calicut in May 1498: a Portuguese ship with a high stern, square sails with a red cross on the fore and main masts and a slanting sail at the back, about twenty-five paces long, coming to anchor under towering monsoon clouds, with a low green coast of coconut palms and blue hills behind it. A long, narrow local fishing boat has come alongside. Three people, each different: on the ship\'s rail a grey-bearded pilot in a turban, the man who showed the ships the way across from Africa, pointing at the shore; beside him a ship\'s boy, thin after ten months at sea, laughing; and in the fishing boat a fisherman of the coast in a waist cloth, standing easily with a paddle and looking up at them. The pilot\'s head is right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. No fort, no cannon fire, no flag being planted. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only cloud there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    breda: { cap: 'Breda, 1667', say: 'bray-DAH', ll: [4.78, 51.59], tx: 'Choose two Domains. They change places. Each one keeps its banner and anything lying on it, and the Figures stay where they are.', fact: 'By the Treaty of Breda in 1667 the Dutch kept the nutmeg island of Run, which they had taken from the English, and the English kept New Amsterdam, which they had taken from the Dutch and named New York.', sure: 3,
      art: 'The peace made at Breda in the Netherlands in July 1667, seen across a table in a room of the castle, at eye level: on the table, pushed toward each other from opposite sides, lie two things that are being exchanged. One is a nutmeg split open, the brown seed in its bright red net, on a small silver dish. The other is a small hand-drawn map of an island at the mouth of a river, with a little walled town at its tip. Three people, each different: an English envoy in a long curled wig and a lace collar, one finger on the map; a Dutch envoy in plain black with a broad white collar, one finger on the dish; and between them a secretary dripping red wax onto a folded paper. Through the window, stepped gable roofs and a canal. The secretary\'s head is right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. No skyscrapers, no flags on the table. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only dark panelling there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    columbian: { cap: 'The Atlantic, from 1492', ll: [-5.99, 37.38], tx: 'The Domains at the two Gates change places, and the Domains at the two Outposts change places. Each one keeps its banner and anything lying on it, and the Figures stay where they are.', fact: 'After 1492 maize, potatoes and tomatoes crossed from the Americas to the rest of the world, and wheat, horses and cattle crossed the other way.', sure: 3,
      art: 'A quay on the river at Seville about 1530, with a ship being loaded and unloaded at the same time by one gangplank. Going down the plank to the shore: baskets of maize cobs, tomatoes, chilli peppers and brown cacao pods, and a live turkey under a sailor\'s arm. Going up the plank into the ship: sacks of wheat, bundles of sugar cane, and a small bay horse led by its halter, in true proportion to the man leading it, its back level with his chest. Three people, each different: the sailor with the turkey, grinning; the groom coaxing the horse; and a merchant in a dark gown and flat cap on the quay, holding a tomato up to the light, puzzled. A twelve-sided stone tower stands on the river bank behind. The merchant\'s head is right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. No soldiers, no gold, no map. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only sky and rigging there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    kudurru: { cap: 'Babylonia, c. 1200 BC', ll: [44.42, 32.54], tx: 'The other player\'s cards cannot replace, move or silence the Domains at your Gate and your Outpost.', fact: 'In Babylonia a grant of land was carved on a stone, with curses on anyone who moved the boundary or took the land.', sure: 3,
      art: 'A Babylonian boundary stone of about 1200 BC, standing in a niche of a mud-brick temple and lit by one clay oil lamp: a smooth, dark, egg-shaped boulder of limestone about half a man\'s height. Round its top are carved the signs of the gods who guard the grant: a sun disc with wavy rays, a crescent moon, an eight-pointed star, a scorpion, and seats with horned crowns resting on them. A long snake is carved down one side from top to bottom. Below the signs the stone is covered with columns of small wedge-shaped writing. No people. Through the temple door behind it, small and bright, lies the land the stone speaks for: a barley field, a canal and a line of date palms. The top of the stone is right of centre, about a fifth of the way down the picture. No Egyptian signs, no winged bulls. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only shadowed brick there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
  };
  Object.keys(P4).forEach(function (k) { T[k] = P4[k]; if (P4[k].say) SAY[k] = P4[k].say; });
